window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"t":"1.1 What is data communication?","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-1.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.1 What is data communication? Data communication is the exchange of data between two or more devices through a transmission medium. For it to work reliably, three things matter: delivery (to the correct destination), accuracy (data arrives unaltered) and timeliness (data arrives soon enough to be useful, which is vital for voice and video)."},{"t":"1.2 Components of a data communication system","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-2.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.2 Components of a data communication system Component Role Example Message The information to send An email, a video Sender Device that originates the message Your laptop Receiver Device that receives the message A web server Medium Physical path of transmission Cable, radio waves Protocol Set of rules both sides follow HTTP, TCP Without a protocol, two devices may be connected but unable to understand each other, just as two people speaking different languages cannot communicate even in the same room. The five components of data communication: a message travels from sender to receiver over a medium, following a protocol"},{"t":"1.3 Data flow (transmission direction)","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-3.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.3 Data flow (transmission direction) Simplex: one direction only (keyboard to computer, TV broadcast). Half-duplex: both directions, but one at a time (walkie-talkie, old hubs). Full-duplex: both directions at once (telephone, modern switched Ethernet). Data flow: simplex is one way only, half-duplex takes turns, full-duplex sends both ways at once"},{"t":"1.4 Analog and digital signals","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-4.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.4 Analog and digital signals Analog signals vary continuously (the human voice, radio waves). Digital signals use discrete levels, typically 0 and 1 (computer data). Digital signals resist noise better because a receiver only needs to decide between two levels. Modulation converts digital data into an analog carrier for transmission over radio or telephone lines; a modem (modulator-demodulator) does this. An analog signal varies smoothly; a digital signal jumps between two levels that stand for 0 and 1"},{"t":"1.5 Serial and parallel transmission","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-5.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.5 Serial and parallel transmission Parallel: several bits sent at once on separate wires. Fast over very short distances, but suffers from skew and cost. Serial: bits sent one after another on one channel. Used for almost all long-distance and modern links (USB, SATA, Ethernet). Synchronous transmission uses a shared clock and sends blocks of data; asynchronous transmission sends characters framed by start and stop bits. Serial transmission sends bits one after another on one channel; parallel sends several bits at once on separate wires"},{"t":"1.6 Transmission media","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-6.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.6 Transmission media Medium Type Typical use Advantages Disadvantages Twisted pair (UTP/STP) Guided LAN cabling (Cat5e, Cat6) Cheap, easy Limited to 100 m, interference Coaxial cable Guided Cable TV, older LANs Better shielding Bulky Optical fibre (single-mode, multi-mode) Guided Backbones, ISPs Very high speed, long distance, immune to EMI Costly, fragile to install Radio waves / Wi-Fi Unguided WLAN, mobile Mobility Interference, security risk Microwave / Satellite Unguided Long-haul, remote areas Wide coverage High latency, weather effects Cross-sections of the three guided media: twisted pair, coaxial cable and optical fibre"},{"t":"1.7 Performance measures","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-7.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.7 Performance measures Bandwidth: maximum capacity of a link (bits per second, for example 100 Mbps). Throughput: the actual rate achieved, always at or below bandwidth. Latency (delay): time for data to travel from source to destination. Made up of propagation, transmission, queuing and processing delay. Jitter: variation in latency, harmful to voice and video. Error rate: proportion of bits received incorrectly. Bandwidth is how much data the link can carry per second; latency is how long data takes to cross it Worked example: a 10 MB file is sent over an 8 Mbps link. 10 MB = 80 Mb, so transmission time = 80 / 8 = 10 seconds (ignoring propagation delay and overhead). Bytes or bits? File sizes are measured in bytes ( B ) but link speeds in bits per second ( b ). One byte is 8 bits, so multiply a file size by 8 before dividing by the link speed."},{"t":"1.8 Error detection basics","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-8.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.8 Error detection basics Noise can flip bits. Common detection methods: parity bit , checksum and Cyclic Redundancy Check (CRC) . Ethernet uses CRC in its frame trailer; if the check fails, the frame is discarded and higher layers (TCP) may retransmit. Error detection with CRC: the sender adds a check value; the receiver recalculates it and discards the frame if they differ"},{"t":"1.9 Multiplexing and switching","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-9.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.9 Multiplexing and switching Multiplexing lets many signals share one link: FDM (by frequency), TDM (by time slot), WDM (by light wavelength on fibre). Circuit switching: a dedicated path is set up for the whole call (traditional telephone). Packet switching: data is split into packets that travel independently and are reassembled (the Internet). It uses links efficiently and tolerates failures. Time-division multiplexing: several signals share one link by taking turns in time slots Circuit switching reserves one path for the whole call; packet switching lets each packet find its own way Check yourself. When you have finished this unit, try the 1.10 hands-on lab and the 1.11 review questions ."},{"t":"1.10 Hands-on lab: Measure bandwidth and latency","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-lab.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.10 Hands-on lab: Measure bandwidth and latency Do these tasks on your own computer or in the lab using free software. Write down what you see at each step: your notes are useful evidence for assessments and for revision. Run ping 8.8.8.8 and note the round-trip time (RTT) in milliseconds. Run a speed test and record download and upload speeds. Compare your measured throughput to the bandwidth your ISP advertises and explain any difference. Tip: use the transfer time calculator in 1.7 to work out how long a 1 GB download should take at your measured speed, then time a real download and compare."},{"t":"1.11 Review: key terms and self-check","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01-review.html","x":"Unit 1 · Data Communication Fundamentals 1.11 Review: key terms and self-check Key terms Make sure you can explain each of these in your own words. The glossary has short definitions. Protocol simplex half-duplex full-duplex modulation bandwidth throughput latency jitter CRC multiplexing packet switching Self-check questions Write your answers first, then open the answers to check them. List the five components of a data communication system. Which transmission mode does modern switched Ethernet use, and why is it better than half-duplex? Why is optical fibre preferred for backbone links? A 25 MB file is sent over a 50 Mbps link. How long does it take in the ideal case? State one difference between circuit switching and packet switching. Show answers Message, sender, receiver, transmission medium, protocol. Full-duplex, so that both devices can send and receive at the same time and no collisions occur, giving higher effective throughput. Very high bandwidth over long distances with low signal loss and immunity to electromagnetic interference. 25 MB = 200 Mb; 200 / 50 = 4 seconds . Circuit switching reserves a dedicated path for the whole communication; packet switching splits data into packets that share the network and may take different routes. Next: when you are confident with this unit, test yourself with the multiple-choice questions for Unit 1 in the Final Revision section."},{"t":"Unit 1: Data Communication Fundamentals","c":"Unit 1","u":"u01.html","x":"Unit 1 Data Communication Fundamentals How data moves between devices: the parts of a communication system, data flow, signals, transmission media, performance, error detection, multiplexing and switching. What you will learn After this unit you can: Define data communication and list its five components. Distinguish transmission modes, data flow directions and signal types. Compare transmission media. Explain bandwidth, throughput, latency and the basic ideas of multiplexing and switching. Topics in this unit 1.1 What is data communication? ✓ 1.2 Components of a data communication system 1 diagram ✓ 1.3 Data flow (transmission direction) 1 diagram ✓ 1.4 Analog and digital signals 1 diagram ✓ 1.5 Serial and parallel transmission 1 diagram ✓ 1.6 Transmission media 1 diagram ✓ 1.7 Performance measures 1 diagram · interactive lab ✓ 1.8 Error detection basics 1 diagram ✓ 1.9 Multiplexing and switching 2 diagrams ✓ 1.10 Hands-on lab: Measure bandwidth and latency ✓ 1.11 Review: key terms and self-check 5 questions with answers ✓"},{"t":"2.1 What is a computer network?","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-1.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.1 What is a computer network? A computer network is a set of devices (hosts) connected by links so that they can share resources and exchange data. Main purposes: Resource sharing: files, printers, internet connection. Communication: email, messaging, voice and video. Centralised management: one place to manage users, security and backups. Reliability and scalability: redundancy and the ability to grow."},{"t":"2.2 Classification by geographic scale","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-2.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.2 Classification by geographic scale Type Full name Range Example PAN Personal Area Network A few metres Bluetooth headset and phone LAN Local Area Network Room, building, campus School computer lab MAN Metropolitan Area Network City City-wide cable or fibre network WAN Wide Area Network Country or world The Internet, bank branch links Networks classified by size: a PAN fits inside a LAN, LANs join into a MAN, and WANs span countries"},{"t":"2.3 Classification by role","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-3.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.3 Classification by role Peer-to-peer (P2P): every computer is both client and server. Cheap and simple, but difficult to secure and manage; suitable for fewer than about ten computers. Client-server: dedicated servers provide services (files, web, email, DNS) to clients . Offers centralised security, backup and control; requires a more skilled administrator and higher cost. This is the model on which the rest of this module is built. Peer-to-peer: every computer is both client and server. Client-server: a dedicated server provides services to clients"},{"t":"2.4 Network topologies","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-4.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.4 Network topologies Topology describes how devices are arranged. Physical topology is the actual cabling layout; logical topology is how data flows. The five common topologies. A switched star is used in almost every modern LAN Topology Description Advantages Disadvantages Bus All devices on one shared cable Cheap, little cable Cable fault stops everyone; collisions Ring Each device connects to two neighbours Orderly access One break can disrupt the ring Star All devices connect to a central switch Easy to manage, fault isolation Central device is a single point of failure Mesh (full or partial) Devices interconnect directly Very reliable Expensive; full mesh needs n(n-1)/2 links Tree / Hybrid Combination of the above Scalable Complex The star topology with a switch is by far the most common in modern LANs. Worked example: how many links does a full mesh of 6 devices need? 6 x 5 / 2 = 15 links ."},{"t":"2.5 The Internet, intranet and extranet","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-5.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.5 The Internet, intranet and extranet Internet: the global network of networks, connected by ISPs and using TCP/IP. Intranet: a private network of an organisation, using Internet technologies, accessible only to its members. Extranet: an intranet partly opened to outside partners (suppliers, customers) under controlled access. The Internet is public, an intranet is private to the organisation, and an extranet gives selected partners controlled access"},{"t":"2.6 Ethernet and LAN technologies","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-6.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.6 Ethernet and LAN technologies Ethernet (IEEE 802.3) is the dominant wired LAN technology. Common standards: Standard Speed Typical medium 10BASE-T 10 Mbps Cat3/Cat5 UTP 100BASE-TX (Fast Ethernet) 100 Mbps Cat5 UTP 1000BASE-T (Gigabit) 1 Gbps Cat5e/Cat6 UTP 10GBASE-T 10 Gbps Cat6a UTP Ethernet originally used CSMA/CD (listen, send, detect collisions). Modern full-duplex switched Ethernet has no collisions. Wireless LAN (IEEE 802.11, Wi-Fi): uses CSMA/CA because wireless devices cannot detect collisions while transmitting. Key concepts: SSID (network name), access point (AP) , channels (2.4 GHz and 5 GHz bands, plus 6 GHz in Wi-Fi 6E), and security modes ( WPA2, WPA3 ; avoid the obsolete WEP). Which Wi-Fi band? 2.4 GHz reaches further and passes through walls better, but it is slower and crowded; 5 GHz and 6 GHz are faster with more channels, but have a shorter range."},{"t":"2.7 Cabling","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-7.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.7 Cabling Twisted pair Ethernet cables are wired to either the T568A or T568B standard. T568A and T568B pin colours. Only the green and orange pairs swap places, so a cable with the same standard at both ends is straight-through Straight-through cable: same standard at both ends. Connects unlike devices (PC to switch). Crossover cable: T568A at one end and T568B at the other. Connects like devices (PC to PC, switch to switch), though most modern devices use Auto-MDIX and do not need it. Maximum UTP segment length is 100 metres ."},{"t":"2.8 Basic network design and structured cabling","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-8.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.8 Basic network design and structured cabling Plan for scalability, reliability, security and cost . A common enterprise design has three layers: access (end devices connect), distribution (policy, routing between VLANs) and core (fast backbone). Small networks collapse these into one or two layers. Use structured cabling: work area, horizontal cabling, patch panel, equipment room, backbone. Three-layer enterprise design: access switches connect devices, distribution switches apply policy, the core is a fast backbone"},{"t":"2.9 Network services at a glance","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-9.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.9 Network services at a glance A typical LAN offers: file and print sharing, DHCP (automatic addressing), DNS (names), web, email, authentication (directory services) and internet access via a gateway. Later units show how to configure each. Check yourself. When you have finished this unit, try the 2.10 hands-on lab and the 2.11 review questions ."},{"t":"2.10 Hands-on lab: Build a small star network","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-lab.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.10 Hands-on lab: Build a small star network Do these tasks on your own computer or in the lab using free software. Write down what you see at each step: your notes are useful evidence for assessments and for revision. In Packet Tracer, place one switch and four PCs; connect them with straight-through cables. Assign IP addresses 192.168.1.1 to 192.168.1.4 with mask 255.255.255.0. Ping between all PCs. Disconnect one PC cable and observe that others still work. Explain why this shows the advantage of the star topology. Sketch the physical and logical topology of the real network at your institution."},{"t":"2.11 Review: key terms and self-check","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02-review.html","x":"Unit 2 · Computer Network Fundamentals 2.11 Review: key terms and self-check Key terms Make sure you can explain each of these in your own words. The glossary has short definitions. Host LAN WAN peer-to-peer client-server topology star mesh Ethernet CSMA/CD CSMA/CA SSID access point straight-through crossover intranet extranet Self-check questions Write your answers first, then open the answers to check them. Name three purposes of a computer network. Give two advantages of client-server over peer-to-peer networks. Why is a star topology preferred in modern LANs? What is its main weakness? How many links are needed for a full mesh of 10 devices? Which cable would you use to connect a switch to another switch on older equipment? Distinguish an intranet from an extranet. Show answers Resource sharing, communication, centralised management/security (also scalability and reliability). Central management and security; easier backup; better scalability and control of resources. Easy to manage and a fault on one cable affects only one device; weakness: the central switch is a single point of failure. 10 x 9 / 2 = 45 links . A crossover cable (modern switches with Auto-MDIX accept a straight-through cable). An intranet is private for an organisation's own users; an extranet extends controlled access to selected outside partners. Next: when you are confident with this unit, test yourself with the multiple-choice questions for Unit 2 in the Final Revision section."},{"t":"Unit 2: Computer Network Fundamentals","c":"Unit 2","u":"u02.html","x":"Unit 2 Computer Network Fundamentals What a network is and how networks are classified by size and role, topologies, the Internet, intranets and extranets, Ethernet and Wi-Fi, cabling and network design. What you will learn After this unit you can: Define a computer network and describe its purposes. Classify networks by size (PAN, LAN, MAN, WAN) and by role (peer-to-peer, client-server). Compare physical and logical topologies. Explain the Internet, intranets and extranets. Describe wired and wireless LAN technologies and basic network design. Topics in this unit 2.1 What is a computer network? ✓ 2.2 Classification by geographic scale 1 diagram ✓ 2.3 Classification by role 1 diagram ✓ 2.4 Network topologies 1 diagram ✓ 2.5 The Internet, intranet and extranet 1 diagram ✓ 2.6 Ethernet and LAN technologies ✓ 2.7 Cabling 1 diagram ✓ 2.8 Basic network design and structured cabling 1 diagram ✓ 2.9 Network services at a glance ✓ 2.10 Hands-on lab: Build a small star network ✓ 2.11 Review: key terms and self-check 6 questions with answers ✓"},{"t":"3.1 Introduction to network devices","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-1.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.1 Introduction to network devices Device OSI layer Function Network Interface Card (NIC) 1-2 Connects a host to the network; has a MAC address Repeater 1 Regenerates a signal to extend distance Hub 1 Repeats incoming bits to all ports (one collision domain); obsolete Bridge 2 Connects two LAN segments, filters by MAC address Switch 2 (some 3) Forwards frames only to the port of the destination MAC; each port is its own collision domain Router 3 Connects different networks and forwards packets by IP address; separates broadcast domains Layer 3 switch 2-3 Switch with routing ability, used between VLANs Wireless Access Point (AP) 1-2 Connects wireless clients to the wired LAN Modem 1 Converts signals for the ISP line (DSL, cable, fibre ONT) Firewall 3-7 Filters traffic by rules Gateway Any Connects networks that use different protocols; commonly the default router Load balancer 4-7 Spreads traffic across several servers Collision domain vs broadcast domain. A collision domain is the area where frames can collide (every hub port shares one; each switch port has its own). A broadcast domain is the area reached by a broadcast frame (a whole switch or VLAN; a router stops broadcasts). A router separates broadcast domains. Each switch port is its own collision domain, but all hub ports share one How a switch learns. A switch reads the source MAC of every frame and records it with the port in its MAC address table . When a frame arrives, it looks up the destination MAC: if known, it sends out that port only (forwarding); if unknown or broadcast, it floods all other ports. A switch learns which MAC address is on which port, then forwards frames only to the right port How a router decides. A router looks at the destination IP of the packet, finds the best match in its routing table , and sends the packet to the next hop. The route used when nothing else matches is the default route (0.0.0.0/0)."},{"t":"3.2 Install and configure network devices","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-2.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.2 Install and configure network devices This section walks through setting up the devices in a small network: planning, first-time access through the console, basic switch and router configuration, VLANs, a wireless access point, and the commands that check your work. The examples use the Cisco IOS command line, which you can practise for free in Cisco Packet Tracer . The small network configured in this section: two PCs, switch SW1, router R1 and a link to the ISP In this section 3.2.1 Planning checklist 3.2.2 Basic switch configuration 3.2.3 Basic router configuration 3.2.4 VLANs and trunk ports 3.2.5 Wireless access point and home router 3.2.6 Verification commands"},{"t":"3.2.1 Planning checklist","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-2-1.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.2.1 Planning checklist Draw the network diagram and list the devices and cables needed. Plan IP addressing (see Unit 4 ) and VLANs if needed. Mount equipment, label cables, connect power and uplinks. Connect to the device console (serial cable and terminal program such as PuTTY at 9600 baud) for first-time setup. Apply configuration, test, save , and document. First-time set-up uses the console port; once an IP address and SSH are configured, the device is managed over the network"},{"t":"3.2.2 Basic switch configuration","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-2-2.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.2.2 Basic switch configuration Cisco IOS CLI Copy Switch> enable Switch# configure terminal Switch(config)# hostname SW1 SW1(config)# enable secret StrongPass123 SW1(config)# service password-encryption SW1(config)# banner motd # Authorized access only # SW1(config)# interface vlan 1 SW1(config-if)# ip address 192.168.1.2 255.255.255.0 SW1(config-if)# no shutdown SW1(config-if)# exit SW1(config)# ip default-gateway 192.168.1.1 SW1(config)# line console 0 SW1(config-line)# password ConPass123 SW1(config-line)# login SW1(config-line)# exit SW1(config)# ip domain-name school.lk SW1(config)# username admin secret AdminPass123 SW1(config)# crypto key generate rsa modulus 2048 SW1(config)# ip ssh version 2 SW1(config)# line vty 0 4 SW1(config-line)# login local SW1(config-line)# transport input ssh SW1(config-line)# end SW1# copy running-config startup-config SSH needs more than transport input ssh . The switch also needs a domain name, an RSA key pair and a local user account, and the VTY lines must use login local so that SSH logins are checked against that user. A plain line password is not enough for SSH."},{"t":"3.2.3 Basic router configuration","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-2-3.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.2.3 Basic router configuration Cisco IOS CLI Copy Router> enable Router# configure terminal Router(config)# hostname R1 R1(config)# interface gigabitEthernet 0/0 R1(config-if)# ip address 192.168.1.1 255.255.255.0 R1(config-if)# no shutdown R1(config-if)# exit R1(config)# interface gigabitEthernet 0/1 R1(config-if)# ip address 10.0.0.1 255.255.255.252 R1(config-if)# no shutdown R1(config-if)# exit R1(config)# ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 10.0.0.2 R1(config)# end R1# copy running-config startup-config R1# show ip route R1# show ip interface brief The prompt tells you where you are: R1> user mode, R1# privileged mode, R1(config)# global configuration, R1(config-if)# interface configuration. exit goes back one level; end returns straight to R1# , where show and copy commands are run."},{"t":"3.2.4 VLANs and trunk ports","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-2-4.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.2.4 VLANs and trunk ports A VLAN divides one physical switch into several logical networks (for example, Staff and Students), improving security and reducing broadcast traffic. VLANs split one switch into separate logical networks; a trunk port carries several VLANs between switches with 802.1Q tags Cisco IOS CLI Copy SW1(config)# vlan 10 SW1(config-vlan)# name STAFF SW1(config-vlan)# exit SW1(config)# vlan 20 SW1(config-vlan)# name STUDENTS SW1(config-vlan)# exit SW1(config)# interface fastEthernet 0/1 SW1(config-if)# switchport mode access SW1(config-if)# switchport access vlan 10 SW1(config-if)# exit SW1(config)# interface fastEthernet 0/24 SW1(config-if)# switchport mode trunk SW1(config-if)# end Ports that carry several VLANs between switches are trunk ports (IEEE 802.1Q tagging)."},{"t":"3.2.5 Wireless access point and home router","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-2-5.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.2.5 Wireless access point and home router Connect to the admin page (for example 192.168.1.1 ) and change the default admin password . Set a meaningful SSID and choose WPA2 or WPA3 with a strong passphrase. Choose the least crowded channel; enable 5 GHz where possible. Disable WPS and remote administration unless needed. Update firmware."},{"t":"3.2.6 Verification commands","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-2-6.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.2.6 Verification commands Command Purpose show running-config View active configuration show mac address-table See learned MAC addresses show vlan brief List VLANs and ports show ip route View routing table show interfaces status Link state of ports"},{"t":"3.3 Network protocols","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-3.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.3 Network protocols A protocol is a set of rules that defines the format, order and actions for communication. Protocols are grouped into a protocol suite ; the Internet uses the TCP/IP suite . Protocol Layer (TCP/IP) Purpose Port HTTP / HTTPS Application Web pages (HTTPS adds TLS encryption) 80 / 443 FTP / SFTP Application File transfer (SFTP is secure, runs over SSH) 21 / 22 SSH Application Secure remote command line 22 Telnet Application Insecure remote command line 23 SMTP Application Sending email 25 / 587 POP3 / IMAP Application Retrieving email 110 / 143 (secure: 995 / 993) DNS Application Name to IP resolution 53 DHCP Application Automatic IP configuration 67 / 68 (UDP) SNMP Application Monitoring network devices 161 (UDP) RDP Application Windows remote desktop 3389 TCP Transport Reliable, connection-oriented delivery - UDP Transport Fast, connectionless delivery - IP (IPv4/IPv6) Internet Logical addressing and routing - ICMP Internet Error and diagnostic messages (ping) - ARP Link/Internet Finds MAC address for an IP address - Ethernet, Wi-Fi Network access Frame delivery on local links - In this section 3.3.1 TCP and UDP 3.3.2 DHCP and the DORA process"},{"t":"3.3.1 TCP and UDP","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-3-1.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.3.1 TCP and UDP Feature TCP UDP Connection Yes (three-way handshake: SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) No Reliability Acknowledgements, retransmission, ordering None Speed / overhead Slower, larger header Faster, small header Used for Web, email, file transfer DNS queries, VoIP, video streaming, games TCP opens every connection with a three-way handshake before any data is sent TCP numbers and acknowledges every segment and resends lost ones; UDP just sends datagrams with no acknowledgements"},{"t":"3.3.2 DHCP and the DORA process","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-3-2.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.3.2 DHCP and the DORA process A client with no address broadcasts D iscover; the server replies O ffer; the client sends R equest; the server sends A cknowledgement carrying the IP address, mask, gateway, DNS server and lease time. DHCP in four messages: Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge (DORA)"},{"t":"3.4 Reference models","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-4.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.4 Reference models A reference model divides communication into layers so that vendors can build interoperable products and engineers can troubleshoot one layer at a time. Analogy: sending data is like posting a letter. You write the letter (application), put it in an envelope with the address (network layer), the post office sorts it (routers) and a van carries it (physical). The receiver reverses every step. In this section 3.4.1 The OSI model 3.4.2 The TCP/IP model and OSI comparison 3.4.3 Encapsulation"},{"t":"3.4.1 The OSI model","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-4-1.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.4.1 The OSI model # Layer Function Data unit (PDU) Examples 7 Application Network services to applications Data HTTP, DNS, SMTP 6 Presentation Format, encryption, compression Data TLS, JPEG, ASCII 5 Session Opens, manages and closes sessions Data NetBIOS, RPC 4 Transport End-to-end delivery, ports, reliability Segment (TCP) / Datagram (UDP) TCP, UDP 3 Network Logical addressing, routing Packet IP, ICMP, routers 2 Data Link Framing, MAC addressing, error detection Frame Ethernet, switches 1 Physical Bits on the medium Bits Cables, hubs, radio Memory aid (top to bottom): A ll P eople S eem T o N eed D ata P rocessing. Data moves down the seven OSI layers at the sender, across the medium as bits, and back up at the receiver"},{"t":"3.4.2 The TCP/IP model and OSI comparison","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-4-2.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.4.2 The TCP/IP model and OSI comparison The TCP/IP model (4 layers) TCP/IP layer Corresponding OSI layers Application 7, 6, 5 Transport 4 Internet 3 Network Access (Link) 2, 1 OSI compared with TCP/IP OSI is a theoretical teaching and troubleshooting model; TCP/IP is the practical model on which the Internet actually runs. OSI has 7 layers and separates presentation and session; TCP/IP has 4 layers and merges them into Application. The four TCP/IP layers mapped onto the seven OSI layers, with example protocols"},{"t":"3.4.3 Encapsulation","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-4-3.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.4.3 Encapsulation When sending, each layer adds its own header: data becomes a segment (transport header with ports), then a packet (IP header with IP addresses), then a frame (MAC header and CRC trailer), then bits. The receiver reverses the process ( de-encapsulation ). Encapsulation: each layer adds its own header (and Ethernet adds a trailer) as data moves down the stack Check yourself. When you have finished this unit, try the 3.5 hands-on lab and the 3.6 review questions ."},{"t":"3.5 Hands-on lab: Devices, VLANs and packet capture","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-lab.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.5 Hands-on lab: Devices, VLANs and packet capture Do these tasks on your own computer or in the lab using free software. Write down what you see at each step: your notes are useful evidence for assessments and for revision. In Packet Tracer build: two PCs on a switch (192.168.1.0/24) and a server in a second network behind a router. Configure the router interfaces and test with ping . Create two VLANs and show that hosts in different VLANs cannot ping each other without a router. Run Wireshark, open a web page, and identify an Ethernet frame, IP packet, TCP segment and HTTP request. Write which OSI layer each belongs to. Capture a DHCP exchange ( ipconfig /release then ipconfig /renew ) and identify the four DORA messages. Useful Wireshark display filters: dhcp (shows the DORA messages), http , dns , tcp.port == 80 and arp . Click a packet to see each layer's header in the middle pane."},{"t":"3.6 Review: key terms and self-check","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03-review.html","x":"Unit 3 · Network Devices and Protocols 3.6 Review: key terms and self-check Key terms Make sure you can explain each of these in your own words. The glossary has short definitions. Switch router MAC table routing table default gateway VLAN trunk collision domain broadcast domain protocol suite TCP UDP handshake DHCP PDU encapsulation Self-check questions Write your answers first, then open the answers to check them. How does a switch differ from a hub? At which OSI layer does a router operate and what address does it use? List the DORA steps of DHCP. Name the PDU at layers 4, 3 and 2. Give two applications that prefer UDP and explain why. Which TCP/IP layer corresponds to OSI layers 5 to 7? Why is Telnet considered insecure, and what replaces it? Show answers A hub repeats frames to all ports (one collision domain, half-duplex); a switch learns MAC addresses and forwards only to the destination port (separate collision domains, full-duplex). Layer 3 (Network); IP address. Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge. Layer 4: segment (TCP) or datagram (UDP); layer 3: packet; layer 2: frame. VoIP and video streaming (also DNS queries, online games): they need low delay and tolerate small loss, so retransmission would hurt. The Application layer. Telnet sends everything, including passwords, in clear text; SSH replaces it with encryption. Next: when you are confident with this unit, test yourself with the multiple-choice questions for Unit 3 in the Final Revision section."},{"t":"Unit 3: Network Devices and Protocols","c":"Unit 3","u":"u03.html","x":"Unit 3 Network Devices and Protocols The devices that build networks and how to configure them, the protocols they use, and the OSI and TCP/IP reference models. What you will learn After this unit you can: Identify common network devices and state the function of each. Install and configure a switch, router, access point and basic firewall. Explain what a network protocol is and describe the most common protocols. Describe the OSI and TCP/IP reference models and map devices and protocols to layers. Topics in this unit 3.1 Introduction to network devices 2 diagrams ✓ 3.2 Install and configure network devices 1 diagram ✓ 3.2.1 Planning checklist 1 diagram ✓ 3.2.2 Basic switch configuration 1 command example ✓ 3.2.3 Basic router configuration 1 command example ✓ 3.2.4 VLANs and trunk ports 1 command example · 1 diagram ✓ 3.2.5 Wireless access point and home router ✓ 3.2.6 Verification commands ✓ 3.3 Network protocols ✓ 3.3.1 TCP and UDP 2 diagrams ✓ 3.3.2 DHCP and the DORA process 1 diagram ✓ 3.4 Reference models ✓ 3.4.1 The OSI model 1 diagram ✓ 3.4.2 The TCP/IP model and OSI comparison 1 diagram ✓ 3.4.3 Encapsulation 1 diagram ✓ 3.5 Hands-on lab: Devices, VLANs and packet capture ✓ 3.6 Review: key terms and self-check 7 questions with answers ✓"},{"t":"4.1 MAC address","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-1.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.1 MAC address A MAC (Media Access Control) address is a 48-bit hardware address burned into a network interface and used for delivery on the local network (Layer 2). Written as 12 hexadecimal digits: 00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E or 00-1A-2B-3C-4D-5E . The first 24 bits are the OUI (Organisationally Unique Identifier) assigned to the manufacturer; the last 24 bits identify the individual card. FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF is the broadcast MAC address. ARP (Address Resolution Protocol) discovers the MAC address that belongs to a known IP address on the same LAN. A MAC address is 48 bits: the first half identifies the manufacturer (OUI), the second half the individual interface Find your MAC address: Windows ipconfig /all (Physical Address); Linux ip link . ARP: the request is broadcast to every host on the LAN; only the owner of the IP address replies with its MAC address"},{"t":"4.2 Number systems (needed for IP addressing)","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-2.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.2 Number systems (needed for IP addressing) Binary uses digits 0 and 1. Each position in an octet (8 bits) has the value: 128 64 32 16 8 4 2 1 Decimal to binary: 192 = 128 + 64 = 11000000 . 172 = 128 + 32 + 8 + 4 = 10101100 . Each bit in an octet has a place value; add the values of the 1 bits to get the decimal number Binary to decimal: 00001010 = 8 + 2 = 10. Hexadecimal (base 16) uses 0-9 and A-F; each hex digit is 4 bits (A = 1010, F = 1111)."},{"t":"4.3 IPv4 addressing","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-3.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.3 IPv4 addressing An IPv4 address is 32 bits, written as four decimal octets (dotted decimal): 192.168.10.25 = 11000000.10101000.00001010.00011001 . It has a network portion and a host portion , separated by the subnet mask . An IPv4 address is 32 bits in four octets; the subnet mask marks which bits are the network part and which are the host part In this section 4.3.1 Classful addressing 4.3.2 Private addresses and NAT 4.3.3 Assignment and delivery types"},{"t":"4.3.1 Classful addressing","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-3-1.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.3.1 Classful addressing Class First octet Default mask Hosts per network Use A 1-126 255.0.0.0 (/8) about 16.7 million Very large networks B 128-191 255.255.0.0 (/16) 65,534 Medium networks C 192-223 255.255.255.0 (/24) 254 Small networks D 224-239 - - Multicast E 240-255 - - Experimental 127.0.0.0/8 is loopback ( 127.0.0.1 refers to the local machine). Historic IPv4 classes by first octet. Classful addressing is no longer used for routing, but the ranges are still useful to recognise Classful addressing was replaced by CIDR in 1993. Today the prefix length (for example /24 ), not the class, decides the size of a network."},{"t":"4.3.2 Private addresses and NAT","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-3-2.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.3.2 Private addresses and NAT Range CIDR 10.0.0.0 - 10.255.255.255 10.0.0.0/8 172.16.0.0 - 172.31.255.255 172.16.0.0/12 192.168.0.0 - 192.168.255.255 192.168.0.0/16 Private addresses are not routed on the Internet; NAT (Network Address Translation) on the router translates them to a public address. APIPA addresses 169.254.x.x appear when a client fails to obtain a DHCP address. NAT: hosts with private addresses share the router's single public address on the Internet"},{"t":"4.3.3 Assignment and delivery types","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-3-3.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.3.3 Assignment and delivery types Static and dynamic assignment Static: set manually; use for servers, routers, printers. Dynamic: leased by a DHCP server; use for clients. Unicast, broadcast, multicast Unicast is one-to-one; broadcast is one-to-all on the subnet (sent to the subnet's broadcast address, which is the last address in the subnet); multicast is one-to-many group. Unicast goes to one host, broadcast to every host on the subnet, multicast to the members of a group"},{"t":"4.4 Subnet masks and CIDR","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-4.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.4 Subnet masks and CIDR The subnet mask has 1s for the network part and 0s for the host part. CIDR notation writes the count of 1s: 255.255.255.0 = /24 . A /26 mask: 26 network bits (1s) followed by 6 host bits (0s), which is 255.255.255.192 CIDR Mask Host bits Usable hosts /24 255.255.255.0 8 254 /25 255.255.255.128 7 126 /26 255.255.255.192 6 62 /27 255.255.255.224 5 30 /28 255.255.255.240 4 14 /29 255.255.255.248 3 6 /30 255.255.255.252 2 2 Formulas: Usable hosts per subnet = 2^h - 2 (h = host bits; subtract the network address and broadcast address). Number of subnets = 2^s (s = bits borrowed from the host part). Block size = 256 - the interesting octet of the mask."},{"t":"4.5 Subnetting step by step","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-5.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.5 Subnetting step by step Worked example 1. Find the network, broadcast and host range for 192.168.10.77/26 . /26 mask = 255.255.255.192. Block size = 256 - 192 = 64 . Subnets in the last octet start at 0, 64, 128, 192. 77 lies between 64 and 128, so the network address = 192.168.10.64 . Broadcast = next network minus 1 = 192.168.10.127. Usable range = 192.168.10.65 to 192.168.10.126 (62 hosts). With a /26 mask the last octet is divided into blocks of 64; 192.168.10.77 falls in the block that starts at 64 Worked example 2 (design). Divide 192.168.1.0/24 into 4 equal subnets. 4 subnets needs 2 bits (2^2 = 4), so the new prefix is /26 (mask 255.255.255.192), 62 hosts each. Subnet Network Usable range Broadcast 1 192.168.1.0/26 .1 - .62 .63 2 192.168.1.64/26 .65 - .126 .127 3 192.168.1.128/26 .129 - .190 .191 4 192.168.1.192/26 .193 - .254 .255 Worked example 3 (requirement-driven). A department needs 50 hosts. Smallest h with 2^h - 2 >= 50 is h = 6 (62), so use /26 . Practise: make up your own addresses, work them out by hand, then check with the subnet calculator in 4.4 . In this section 4.5.1 VLSM and the default gateway"},{"t":"4.5.1 VLSM and the default gateway","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-5-1.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.5.1 VLSM and the default gateway VLSM (Variable Length Subnet Masking) VLSM lets you use different prefix lengths so that no addresses are wasted. Procedure: sort requirements from largest to smallest and allocate in that order. Example: from 192.168.5.0/24 provide LAN A (100 hosts), LAN B (50 hosts), LAN C (20 hosts), and a router link (2 hosts). LAN A: 100 hosts needs /25 → 192.168.5.0/25 (0-127) LAN B: 50 hosts needs /26 → 192.168.5.128/26 (128-191) LAN C: 20 hosts needs /27 → 192.168.5.192/27 (192-223) Link: 2 hosts needs /30 → 192.168.5.224/30 (224-227) VLSM plan for 192.168.5.0/24: each subnet is just big enough, largest first, leaving space for future growth Default gateway To reach any address outside its own subnet, a host sends traffic to its default gateway (the router interface on its subnet). A wrong gateway or mask is among the most common causes of connectivity faults."},{"t":"4.6 IPv6 addressing","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-6.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.6 IPv6 addressing IPv4 has about 4.3 billion addresses, which are exhausted. IPv6 uses 128-bit addresses written as eight groups of four hex digits: 2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:ff00:0042:8329 . Shortening rules: drop leading zeros in a group; replace one run of all-zero groups with :: (only once). The address above becomes 2001:db8::ff00:42:8329 . Shortening an IPv6 address: drop leading zeros in each group, then replace one run of all-zero groups with :: Type Prefix Note Global unicast 2000::/3 Public, routable Link-local fe80::/10 Automatically on every interface, local link only Unique local fc00::/7 Private-style addressing Multicast ff00::/8 Replaces broadcast Loopback ::1 Local host IPv6 has no broadcast and usually uses a /64 prefix for a LAN. Addresses can be assigned by SLAAC (auto-configuration) or DHCPv6. Transition methods: dual-stack, tunnelling, translation. Check yourself. When you have finished this unit, try the 4.7 hands-on lab and the 4.8 review questions ."},{"t":"4.7 Hands-on lab: Addressing and subnetting","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-lab.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.7 Hands-on lab: Addressing and subnetting Do these tasks on your own computer or in the lab using free software. Write down what you see at each step: your notes are useful evidence for assessments and for revision. Record the MAC address, IPv4 address, mask, gateway and DNS of your computer ( ipconfig /all or ip a ). Run arp -a and match entries to devices on your LAN. Subnet 172.16.0.0/16 into 8 equal subnets; list network, range and broadcast of each. In Packet Tracer, configure two subnets joined by a router and prove connectivity with ping and tracert . Convert five IPv4 addresses to binary by hand and check with a calculator. Check your work with the subnet calculator , the VLSM planner and the binary converter , but only after you have worked each answer out by hand."},{"t":"4.8 Review: key terms and self-check","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04-review.html","x":"Unit 4 · Addressing Network Devices 4.8 Review: key terms and self-check Key terms Make sure you can explain each of these in your own words. The glossary has short definitions. MAC OUI ARP octet subnet mask CIDR VLSM private address NAT APIPA default gateway IPv6 link-local SLAAC Self-check questions Write your answers first, then open the answers to check them. How many bits are in a MAC address and an IPv4 address? Convert 203 to binary and 10010110 to decimal. Which of these are private: 172.20.5.1, 172.32.0.1, 192.168.99.5, 11.0.0.1? For 10.1.1.200/27 find network, broadcast and usable range. How many usable hosts are in a /28? What mask do you need for at least 500 hosts? Shorten fe80:0000:0000:0000:02aa:00ff:fe28:9c5a . What does ARP do? Show answers MAC 48 bits; IPv4 32 bits. 203 = 128+64+8+2+1 = 11001011 ; 10010110 = 128+16+4+2 = 150 . 172.20.5.1 and 192.168.99.5 are private. 172.32.0.1 is outside 172.16-172.31 and 11.0.0.1 is public. /27 block size 32; 200 lies in 192-223: network 10.1.1.192 , broadcast 10.1.1.223 , usable 10.1.1.193 - 10.1.1.222 . 2^4 - 2 = 14 . 2^9 - 2 = 510 >= 500, so 9 host bits, mask /23 (255.255.254.0). fe80::2aa:ff:fe28:9c5a . ARP finds the MAC address that corresponds to a known IP address on the local network. Next: when you are confident with this unit, test yourself with the multiple-choice questions for Unit 4 in the Final Revision section."},{"t":"Unit 4: Addressing Network Devices","c":"Unit 4","u":"u04.html","x":"Unit 4 Addressing Network Devices How devices are addressed: MAC addresses, binary, IPv4 classes and private ranges, subnet masks, subnetting, VLSM and IPv6. What you will learn After this unit you can: Describe the structure and purpose of a MAC address. Explain IPv4 and IPv6 addressing, classes, public and private ranges. Convert between binary and decimal. Design and calculate subnets using subnet masks, CIDR and VLSM. Topics in this unit 4.1 MAC address 2 diagrams ✓ 4.2 Number systems (needed for IP addressing) 1 diagram · interactive lab ✓ 4.3 IPv4 addressing 1 diagram ✓ 4.3.1 Classful addressing 1 diagram ✓ 4.3.2 Private addresses and NAT 1 diagram ✓ 4.3.3 Assignment and delivery types 1 diagram ✓ 4.4 Subnet masks and CIDR 1 diagram · interactive lab ✓ 4.5 Subnetting step by step 1 diagram ✓ 4.5.1 VLSM and the default gateway 1 diagram · interactive lab ✓ 4.6 IPv6 addressing 1 diagram · interactive lab ✓ 4.7 Hands-on lab: Addressing and subnetting ✓ 4.8 Review: key terms and self-check 8 questions with answers ✓"},{"t":"5.1 What is a server and why is a server OS required?","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-1.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.1 What is a server and why is a server OS required? A server is a computer (or software) that provides services to other computers (clients) over a network. A server must run continuously , serve many users at once , be secure and be manageable remotely . The server OS manages the hardware; server applications on top of it provide the services clients use Server OS compared with a desktop OS Feature Desktop OS Server OS Purpose One interactive user Many simultaneous remote users and services Hardware support Consumer hardware Multi-CPU, large RAM, RAID, hot-swap, ECC memory Roles / services Few DNS, DHCP, web, file, directory, database, mail and more Uptime and stability Moderate Designed for months or years without reboot Management Mostly local GUI Remote tools, command line, scripting, centralised policy Security Basic Fine-grained permissions, auditing, role-based access Licensing Per device Server licence plus client access licences (CALs) for Windows; free/open source or subscription for Linux Why application software is needed The operating system manages hardware; server application software delivers the actual service: a web server (Apache, Nginx, IIS), database (MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server), mail server (Postfix, Exchange), file server (Samba, SMB), DNS (BIND, Windows DNS). A server's purpose is defined by the applications installed, so the operating system and application stack must be chosen together to match the workload."},{"t":"5.2 Types of servers (by role)","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-2.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.2 Types of servers (by role) File server, print server, web server, application server, database server, mail server, DNS server, DHCP server, proxy server, authentication/directory server (Active Directory, LDAP), backup server, and virtualization host. One computer can play many server roles; the role is defined by the server application installed"},{"t":"5.3 Server hosting and deployment options","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-3.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.3 Server hosting and deployment options Hosting options from cheapest and least work to most control and responsibility Option Description Advantages Disadvantages Typical use Dedicated server A whole physical machine for one customer Full performance, control and isolation Expensive, you manage hardware or pay for it Large databases, high-traffic sites Shared hosting Many customers share one server and its resources Very cheap, no server management Limited control, neighbours can slow you, limited security Small websites, blogs Virtual Private Server (VPS) One physical machine divided into isolated virtual machines, each with its own OS and root access Good balance of cost and control, scalable Shares hardware, performance can vary Small to medium business apps Cloud server Virtual servers in a provider data centre, billed by use (AWS, Azure, Google Cloud) Elastic scaling, high availability, pay as you go Costs can grow, depends on provider Variable or global workloads On-premises server Owned and run in your own server room Full control of data Capital cost, power, cooling, staff Sensitive data, local services Colocation Your own server hosted in a provider's data centre Provider power, cooling and bandwidth Hardware cost remains with you Companies needing own hardware In this section 5.3.1 Selecting a server"},{"t":"5.3.1 Selecting a server","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-3-1.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.3.1 Selecting a server Consider: workload (users, transactions, data volume), CPU cores, RAM, storage (SSD vs HDD, RAID level), network bandwidth , availability (redundant power supplies, clustering, backups), security and compliance , scalability , budget (purchase plus running cost), support and warranty , and skills available . Basic RAID levels: RAID 0 (striping, speed, no protection), RAID 1 (mirroring), RAID 5 (striping with parity, survives one disk failure), RAID 6 (two disk failures), RAID 10 (mirror plus stripe). RAID 0 stripes for speed only, RAID 1 mirrors, RAID 5 stripes with parity so any one disk can fail RAID is not a backup. It keeps a server running when a disk fails, but deleted files, ransomware and fire affect every disk at once. Keep separate backups. Worked example: a school needs a web server for 500 students and a file server for 50 staff. A virtual machine on one on-premises host or a small cloud VPS (2 vCPU, 4 GB RAM) is enough for the web server; the file server needs mirrored disks and daily backup. A dedicated server would be wasteful."},{"t":"5.4 Server operating systems","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-4.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.4 Server operating systems OS Key features Typical roles Windows Server (2019, 2022, 2025) GUI, Active Directory, Group Policy, IIS, PowerShell; licensed Domain services, file/print, Exchange Linux (Ubuntu Server, Debian, Red Hat Enterprise Linux, Rocky/AlmaLinux, SUSE) Free or subscription, stable, command line, low resource use Web, DNS, database, containers, cloud Unix (FreeBSD, Solaris, AIX) Very stable, specialised Legacy, appliances, enterprise Hypervisors (VMware ESXi, Hyper-V, KVM, Proxmox) Run many virtual machines Virtualization host In this section 5.4.1 Installation procedure 5.4.2 Practical: Ubuntu Server and a static IP 5.4.3 Windows Server first tasks"},{"t":"5.4.1 Installation procedure","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-4-1.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.4.1 Installation procedure 1 Plan → 2 Back up → 3 Prepare media → 4 Boot → 5 Partition disks → 6 Admin account → 7 Network → 8 First boot tasks → 9 Document Plan: check hardware requirements, choose edition, note licence key, plan hostname, static IP, disk layout. Back up any existing data. Prepare media: download the ISO, verify its checksum , write to USB or attach to the virtual machine. Boot from media ; choose language, keyboard and installation type (use Server Core / minimal where a GUI is not needed, which reduces attack surface). Partition disks (for example separate / , /var , /home on Linux; system and data volumes on Windows). Consider RAID. Set administrator account and a strong password. Configure network: static IP, mask, gateway, DNS. First boot tasks: set hostname and time zone/NTP, apply all updates, enable firewall, remove unneeded services, create a standard user for daily tasks. Back up the clean configuration and document it."},{"t":"5.4.2 Practical: Ubuntu Server and a static IP","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-4-2.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.4.2 Practical: Ubuntu Server and a static IP Practical: install Ubuntu Server in VirtualBox Create a VM: 2 CPUs, 2 GB RAM, 20 GB disk, network in Bridged or NAT with port forwarding. Mount the Ubuntu Server ISO and start the VM. Follow the installer; tick Install OpenSSH server . After reboot log in and run: Terminal Copy sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y hostnamectl set-hostname server1 ip a Setting a static IP on Ubuntu (Netplan) Edit the YAML file in /etc/netplan/ (for example 00-installer-config.yaml or 50-cloud-init.yaml ; the name depends on the Ubuntu version): YAML Copy network: version: 2 ethernets: enp0s3: dhcp4: false addresses: [192.168.1.10/24] routes: - to: default via: 192.168.1.1 nameservers: addresses: [8.8.8.8, 1.1.1.1] Test the change with sudo netplan try (it rolls back automatically if you lose the connection), then make it permanent with sudo netplan apply . YAML indentation matters: use spaces, never tabs. The interface name ( enp0s3 here) must match the name shown by ip a ."},{"t":"5.4.3 Windows Server first tasks","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-4-3.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.4.3 Windows Server first tasks Use Server Manager to rename the computer, set a static IP (Network Connections), run Windows Update, and add roles with Add Roles and Features (or PowerShell: Install-WindowsFeature DNS -IncludeManagementTools ). PowerShell Copy Rename-Computer -NewName SRV1 -Restart New-NetIPAddress -InterfaceAlias \"Ethernet\" -IPAddress 192.168.1.20 -PrefixLength 24 -DefaultGateway 192.168.1.1 Set-DnsClientServerAddress -InterfaceAlias \"Ethernet\" -ServerAddresses 192.168.1.10 Install-WindowsFeature DNS, DHCP -IncludeManagementTools Get-WindowsFeature | Where-Object Installed These PowerShell commands do the same jobs as Server Manager. Change the interface name ( Get-NetAdapter lists them) and the addresses to suit your network."},{"t":"5.5 Server access methodologies","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-5.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.5 Server access methodologies Method Description Port Notes Local console Keyboard, monitor, mouse on the server - Needs physical presence SSH Encrypted remote command line (Linux, network devices) 22 Use key-based login; disable root login RDP Remote graphical desktop for Windows 3389 Use VPN, Network Level Authentication, strong passwords PowerShell Remoting / WinRM Remote scripting for Windows 5985/5986 Good for automation Telnet Unencrypted command line 23 Do not use Web-based panels (cPanel, Webmin, Cockpit) Manage through a browser various Protect with HTTPS and restricted access Out-of-band (iLO, iDRAC, IPMI, KVM over IP) Hardware management even when the OS is down - Place on a separate management network File transfer (SFTP, SCP, SMB) Move files 22 / 445 Prefer SFTP/SCP SSH example: Terminal Copy ssh user@192.168.1.10 ssh-keygen -t ed25519 # create a key pair on your PC ssh-copy-id user@192.168.1.10 # install your public key on the server scp report.pdf user@192.168.1.10:/home/user/ Key-based SSH: the public key is copied to the server, and the private key never leaves your computer Good practice: disable password login once keys work ( PasswordAuthentication no in /etc/ssh/sshd_config ), use least-privilege accounts, and log all administrative sessions."},{"t":"5.6 Server applications and tools","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-6.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.6 Server applications and tools Common server applications Service Linux Windows Web Apache, Nginx IIS Database MySQL/MariaDB, PostgreSQL SQL Server File sharing Samba, NFS File Server role (SMB) DHCP isc-dhcp-server, dnsmasq DHCP Server role DNS BIND9 DNS Server role Mail Postfix, Dovecot Exchange Directory OpenLDAP, FreeIPA Active Directory Domain Services Install and test a web server (Ubuntu): Terminal Copy sudo apt install apache2 -y systemctl status apache2 curl http://localhost Open a browser on another computer to http://server-ip ; the default Apache page confirms success. Content lives in /var/www/html . Administration tools Task Linux Windows Manage services systemctl start/stop/enable Services console, Get-Service View processes top , htop , ps Task Manager, Get-Process Disk usage df -h , du -sh Disk Management, Storage Spaces Users and groups adduser , usermod , chmod , chown Active Directory Users and Computers, Local Users Logs /var/log , journalctl Event Viewer Packages and updates apt , dnf Windows Update, WSUS Scheduling cron Task Scheduler Monitoring Nagios, Zabbix, Prometheus Performance Monitor, SCOM Backup rsync , tar , Bacula Windows Server Backup Linux permissions: chmod 750 file gives owner rwx (7), group r-x (5), others none (0)."},{"t":"5.7 Ports","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-7.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.7 Ports An IP address identifies a host; a port number (16 bits, 0-65535) identifies the application or service on that host. A socket is IP address plus port (for example 192.168.1.10:443 ). Ports can be TCP or UDP . A socket is an IP address plus a port. The server listens on one port per service; the firewall blocks ports that should not be public Range Name Use 0-1023 Well-known ports Standard services 1024-49151 Registered ports Applications (MySQL 3306, RDP 3389) 49152-65535 Dynamic / ephemeral Temporary client-side ports Port Service 20/21 FTP data / control 22 SSH, SFTP 23 Telnet 25 SMTP 53 DNS 67/68 DHCP 80 HTTP 110 POP3 143 IMAP 389 / 636 LDAP / LDAPS 443 HTTPS 445 SMB 3306 MySQL 3389 RDP Checking and securing ports Terminal Copy sudo ss -tulpn # Linux: list listening ports and their programs netstat -ano # Windows: connections with process IDs nmap -sT 192.168.1.10 # scan a server from another machine (only your own!) Security rule: open only the ports a server needs ; close or firewall all others. Unused open ports are attack entry points. Port forwarding on a router maps a public port to an internal server port (for example public 8080 to 192.168.1.10:80). Check yourself. When you have finished this unit, try the 5.8 hands-on lab and the 5.9 review questions ."},{"t":"5.8 Hands-on lab: Build a Linux server","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-lab.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.8 Hands-on lab: Build a Linux server Do these tasks on your own computer or in the lab using free software. Write down what you see at each step: your notes are useful evidence for assessments and for revision. Install Ubuntu Server in a VM, set a static IP, install SSH and connect from your host. Install Apache, change the default page, and view it from another computer. Create a user, set file permissions and test access. List listening ports with ss -tulpn and justify each one. Install a Windows Server evaluation VM and add the DHCP and DNS roles. Take a VM snapshot after each working step (installed, static IP, SSH, Apache). If a later change breaks the server, roll back in seconds instead of reinstalling."},{"t":"5.9 Review: key terms and self-check","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05-review.html","x":"Unit 5 · Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration 5.9 Review: key terms and self-check Key terms Make sure you can explain each of these in your own words. The glossary has short definitions. Server client dedicated shared VPS cloud hypervisor RAID Server Core SSH RDP port socket service daemon role package manager Self-check questions Write your answers first, then open the answers to check them. Give three differences between a desktop OS and a server OS. Compare shared hosting and a VPS. A company needs a server for a busy online shop with traffic spikes. Which option would you suggest and why? Which port and protocol are used by SSH, HTTPS and DNS? Why should password login be replaced by key-based login on SSH? What does the command ss -tulpn show? State two post-installation tasks you must do on every new server. Show answers A server OS supports multiple simultaneous remote users and services, offers roles/services such as DNS and AD, has stronger remote management and security, supports server-grade hardware and is built for continuous uptime. (Any three.) Shared: many sites on one OS, no root access, cheapest and limited control. VPS: isolated virtual machine with its own OS and root access, more resources and flexibility, somewhat higher cost. A cloud server or auto-scaling VPS cluster, because it can scale up for traffic spikes and be billed by use; add load balancing and backups. SSH 22 (TCP); HTTPS 443 (TCP); DNS 53 (UDP, TCP for large replies/zone transfers). Keys are far harder to guess or brute-force than passwords and cannot be captured by typing; password login can then be disabled. It lists listening TCP/UDP ports and the programs using them. Apply updates, set a static IP/hostname, enable firewall, create a non-admin account, disable unneeded services, take a backup (any two). Next: when you are confident with this unit, test yourself with the multiple-choice questions for Unit 5 in the Final Revision section."},{"t":"Unit 5: Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration","c":"Unit 5","u":"u05.html","x":"Unit 5 Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration Why servers need a server OS, hosting options and server selection, installing Linux and Windows servers, remote access, server applications and ports. What you will learn After this unit you can: Explain what a server is and why server operating systems and application software are required. Compare dedicated, shared, VPS, cloud and virtual servers and select a suitable one. Install and configure a server operating system. Describe and use server access methods. Install common server applications and tools. Explain ports and manage them securely. Topics in this unit 5.1 What is a server and why is a server OS required? 1 diagram ✓ 5.2 Types of servers (by role) 1 diagram ✓ 5.3 Server hosting and deployment options 1 diagram ✓ 5.3.1 Selecting a server 1 diagram ✓ 5.4 Server operating systems ✓ 5.4.1 Installation procedure ✓ 5.4.2 Practical: Ubuntu Server and a static IP 2 command examples ✓ 5.4.3 Windows Server first tasks 1 command example ✓ 5.5 Server access methodologies 1 command example · 1 diagram ✓ 5.6 Server applications and tools 1 command example ✓ 5.7 Ports 1 command example · 1 diagram · interactive lab ✓ 5.8 Hands-on lab: Build a Linux server ✓ 5.9 Review: key terms and self-check 7 questions with answers ✓"},{"t":"6.1 Concept","c":"Unit 6","u":"u06-1.html","x":"Unit 6 · Virtual Private Network (VPN) 6.1 Concept A Virtual Private Network creates an encrypted tunnel across an untrusted network (the Internet) so that remote users and sites can communicate as if on one private network. It provides: Confidentiality: data is encrypted. Integrity: data cannot be altered unnoticed. Authentication: only authorised users or devices connect. A VPN builds an encrypted tunnel across the untrusted Internet, so the remote user works as if inside the office network"},{"t":"6.2 Types of VPN","c":"Unit 6","u":"u06-2.html","x":"Unit 6 · Virtual Private Network (VPN) 6.2 Types of VPN Type Description Example Remote-access VPN A single user connects to the organisation network from outside Staff working from home using a VPN client Site-to-site VPN Two networks (branch and head office) joined permanently by VPN gateways or routers Bank branch to head office Client-based vs clientless (SSL/browser) Software client or browser-only access SSL VPN portal Commercial / personal VPN Service used to protect traffic on public Wi-Fi or change apparent location Consumer VPN apps Remote-access VPNs connect individual users; site-to-site VPNs permanently join whole networks"},{"t":"6.3 Tunnelling and encapsulation","c":"Unit 6","u":"u06-3.html","x":"Unit 6 · Virtual Private Network (VPN) 6.3 Tunnelling and encapsulation In tunnelling, the original packet is encrypted and wrapped inside another packet with new outer IP addresses (the VPN endpoints). Routers on the Internet see only the outer header. At the far end the packet is decrypted and delivered to the private network. Tunnelling: the original packet is encrypted and wrapped in a new packet addressed between the two VPN endpoints"},{"t":"6.4 VPN protocols","c":"Unit 6","u":"u06-4.html","x":"Unit 6 · Virtual Private Network (VPN) 6.4 VPN protocols Protocol Notes IPsec (IKE + ESP/AH) Network-layer standard; common for site-to-site; modes: transport and tunnel SSL/TLS VPN (e.g. OpenVPN) Uses TLS, works over TCP/UDP 443 or 1194, passes through firewalls easily WireGuard Modern, small and fast, uses UDP (default 51820) L2TP/IPsec Tunnel with IPsec encryption PPTP Obsolete and insecure; avoid"},{"t":"6.5 Benefits and limits","c":"Unit 6","u":"u06-5.html","x":"Unit 6 · Virtual Private Network (VPN) 6.5 Benefits and limits Benefits: secure remote work, lower cost than leased lines, secure branch connectivity, protection on public Wi-Fi. Limits: extra latency and processing, requires correct configuration and key management, a compromised VPN client can bring a threat inside the network, and a VPN does not protect against malware or phishing. Best practice: use strong authentication (certificates plus multi-factor), keep VPN software updated, apply least-privilege access and log connections."},{"t":"6.6 Quick set-up outline (WireGuard on Ubuntu)","c":"Unit 6","u":"u06-6.html","x":"Unit 6 · Virtual Private Network (VPN) 6.6 Quick set-up outline (WireGuard on Ubuntu) Terminal Copy sudo apt install wireguard -y umask 077 # new files readable only by you wg genkey | tee server.key | wg pubkey > server.pub sudo nano /etc/wireguard/wg0.conf # add the [Interface] and [Peer] sections shown below sudo wg-quick up wg0 sudo wg show The [Interface] section holds the server's private key, tunnel address (for example 10.8.0.1/24 ) and listening port; each [Peer] holds a client's public key and allowed IP. Open the UDP port on the firewall ( sudo ufw allow 51820/udp ) and, if clients must reach the LAN behind the server, enable IP forwarding ( net.ipv4.ip_forward=1 in /etc/sysctl.conf ). Configuration file Copy [Interface] Address = 10.8.0.1/24 ListenPort = 51820 PrivateKey = <contents of server.key> [Peer] PublicKey = <the client's public key> AllowedIPs = 10.8.0.2/32 A WireGuard server on UDP 51820 gives each peer a tunnel address in 10.8.0.0/24 and forwards its traffic to the private LAN Check yourself. When you have finished this unit, try the 6.7 hands-on lab and the 6.8 review questions ."},{"t":"6.7 Hands-on lab: Build a VPN","c":"Unit 6","u":"u06-lab.html","x":"Unit 6 · Virtual Private Network (VPN) 6.7 Hands-on lab: Build a VPN Do these tasks on your own computer or in the lab using free software. Write down what you see at each step: your notes are useful evidence for assessments and for revision. Build a WireGuard or OpenVPN server in a VM and connect a second VM or phone as a client. Confirm the client receives a tunnel IP and can reach a private server ( ping 10.8.0.1 ). Use Wireshark to show that traffic on the physical interface is encrypted. In Wireshark, capture on the physical network adapter while you ping through the tunnel: you should see only UDP packets to port 51820 (WireGuard) with unreadable contents, not the ICMP pings."},{"t":"6.8 Review: key terms and self-check","c":"Unit 6","u":"u06-review.html","x":"Unit 6 · Virtual Private Network (VPN) 6.8 Review: key terms and self-check Key terms Make sure you can explain each of these in your own words. The glossary has short definitions. Tunnel IPsec TLS remote-access VPN site-to-site VPN encapsulation authentication key Self-check questions Write your answers first, then open the answers to check them. Define a VPN and give its three security benefits. Differentiate remote-access and site-to-site VPNs. Why is PPTP no longer recommended? Does a VPN make a malware-infected computer safe? Explain. Show answers A VPN is an encrypted tunnel across an untrusted network; benefits are confidentiality, integrity and authentication. Remote-access connects individual users to a network; site-to-site joins two networks through gateways. PPTP's authentication (MS-CHAPv2) and encryption (MPPE, based on RC4) have well-known weaknesses: a captured login can be cracked quickly and the traffic then decrypted. It is obsolete and should be replaced by WireGuard, OpenVPN or IPsec (IKEv2). No. A VPN protects data in transit only; the infected computer can still spread malware through the tunnel. Next: when you are confident with this unit, test yourself with the multiple-choice questions for Unit 6 in the Final Revision section."},{"t":"Unit 6: Virtual Private Network (VPN)","c":"Unit 6","u":"u06.html","x":"Unit 6 Virtual Private Network (VPN) Virtual private networks: why they are used, remote-access and site-to-site VPNs, tunnelling, VPN protocols and a WireGuard set-up. What you will learn After this unit you can: Explain what a VPN is and why it is used. Compare site-to-site and remote-access VPNs. Describe tunnelling and common VPN protocols. Set up a simple VPN. Topics in this unit 6.1 Concept 1 diagram ✓ 6.2 Types of VPN 1 diagram ✓ 6.3 Tunnelling and encapsulation 1 diagram ✓ 6.4 VPN protocols ✓ 6.5 Benefits and limits ✓ 6.6 Quick set-up outline (WireGuard on Ubuntu) 2 command examples · 1 diagram ✓ 6.7 Hands-on lab: Build a VPN ✓ 6.8 Review: key terms and self-check 4 questions with answers ✓"},{"t":"7.1 Why DNS?","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-1.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.1 Why DNS? People remember names ( www.example.com ), computers route by IP addresses ( 93.184.216.34 ). DNS is a distributed, hierarchical database that translates names to IP addresses (and back), and it also stores mail routing and other information. DNS translates the name people type into the IP address computers use, before the real connection starts"},{"t":"7.2 DNS architecture","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-2.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.2 DNS architecture The namespace is an inverted tree : Root (.) : 13 logical root server addresses (A to M), operated by many organisations with many mirrored instances. Top-Level Domains (TLD): generic ( .com , .org , .net ), country-code ( .lk , .uk , .in ) and newer generic TLDs such as .app and .shop . Second-level domain: the registered name ( example in example.com ). Subdomain / host: www , mail and so on. The DNS namespace is an inverted tree: the root, top-level domains, second-level domains, then hosts and subdomains A Fully Qualified Domain Name (FQDN) names a host completely: www.example.com. (the trailing dot is the root). A zone is the portion of the namespace administered by one set of name servers; a zone file holds its records. DNS server roles Role Function Authoritative server Holds the official records for a zone (primary/master and secondary/slave, kept in sync by zone transfer ) Recursive resolver Asks other servers on the client's behalf and caches answers (your ISP, 8.8.8.8, 1.1.1.1) Caching server Stores answers for the record's TTL to speed up repeated lookups Forwarder Passes queries it cannot answer to another resolver Root and TLD servers Direct resolvers down the tree"},{"t":"7.3 Name resolution step by step","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-3.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.3 Name resolution step by step A client wants www.example.com : The client checks its own cache and the hosts file. It asks its configured recursive resolver . If not cached, the resolver asks a root server , which refers it to the .com TLD servers. The TLD server refers it to the authoritative server for example.com . The authoritative server returns the IP address. The resolver caches the answer (for the TTL) and returns it to the client. Resolving www.example.com with an empty cache: the resolver is referred from the root to the TLD to the authoritative server Queries use UDP port 53 (TCP 53 for large responses and zone transfers). Recursive queries ask the server to find the complete answer; iterative queries accept a referral."},{"t":"7.4 DNS record types","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-4.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.4 DNS record types Record Purpose Example A Name to IPv4 address www IN A 192.0.2.10 AAAA Name to IPv6 address www IN AAAA 2001:db8::10 CNAME Alias to another name web IN CNAME www.example.com. MX Mail server for the domain (with priority) @ IN MX 10 mail.example.com. NS Name servers for the zone @ IN NS ns1.example.com. PTR IP address to name (reverse lookup) 10 IN PTR www.example.com. SOA Start of Authority: primary server, serial, refresh timers one per zone TXT Free text; used for SPF, DKIM, domain verification \"v=spf1 mx -all\" SRV Location of a service used by Active Directory, SIP The trailing dot matters. In a zone file mail.example.com. is a complete name. Without the final dot BIND adds the zone name, so mail.example.com becomes mail.example.com.example.com."},{"t":"7.5 Configuring a DNS server","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-5.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.5 Configuring a DNS server A DNS server can be built on Linux with BIND9 or on Windows Server with the DNS Server role. The pages below configure the same example zone, school.lk , on both, then add a reverse zone, test the server and protect it. In this section 7.5.1 BIND9 on Ubuntu 7.5.2 Windows Server DNS 7.5.3 Reverse zones, management and testing 7.5.4 DNS attacks and protection"},{"t":"7.5.1 BIND9 on Ubuntu","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-5-1.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.5.1 BIND9 on Ubuntu Terminal Copy sudo apt install bind9 bind9utils -y In /etc/bind/named.conf.local declare the zone: Configuration file Copy zone \"school.lk\" { type master; file \"/etc/bind/db.school.lk\"; }; Create /etc/bind/db.school.lk : Newer BIND versions also accept type primary; , the modern name for type master; . DNS zone file Copy $TTL 3600 @ IN SOA ns1.school.lk. admin.school.lk. ( 2025010101 ; serial 3600 ; refresh 900 ; retry 604800 ; expire 3600 ) ; negative cache TTL @ IN NS ns1.school.lk. ns1 IN A 192.168.1.10 www IN A 192.168.1.20 mail IN A 192.168.1.25 @ IN MX 10 mail.school.lk. Check and apply: Terminal Copy sudo named-checkconf sudo named-checkzone school.lk /etc/bind/db.school.lk sudo systemctl restart bind9 Remember to increase the serial number whenever you edit the zone. Also allow DNS in the firewall ( sudo ufw allow 53 )."},{"t":"7.5.2 Windows Server DNS","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-5-2.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.5.2 Windows Server DNS Server Manager > Add Roles and Features > DNS Server . Open DNS Manager , right-click Forward Lookup Zones > New Zone > Primary zone > name school.lk . Right-click the zone > New Host (A) to add records; tick Create associated PTR record if a reverse zone exists. Configure Forwarders (for example 8.8.8.8) on the server properties. Point clients' DNS setting to this server (usually through DHCP)."},{"t":"7.5.3 Reverse zones, management and testing","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-5-3.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.5.3 Reverse zones, management and testing Reverse lookup zones For the 192.168.1.0/24 network the reverse zone is 1.168.192.in-addr.arpa ; PTR records map the last octet to a name. A reverse lookup writes the network octets backwards under in-addr.arpa; a PTR record maps the address back to a name Configuration file Copy zone \"1.168.192.in-addr.arpa\" { type master; file \"/etc/bind/db.192.168.1\"; }; DNS zone file Copy $TTL 3600 @ IN SOA ns1.school.lk. admin.school.lk. ( 2025010101 3600 900 604800 3600 ) @ IN NS ns1.school.lk. 10 IN PTR ns1.school.lk. 20 IN PTR www.school.lk. 25 IN PTR mail.school.lk. Managing and testing DNS Terminal Copy nslookup www.school.lk 192.168.1.10 dig www.school.lk @192.168.1.10 dig MX school.lk ipconfig /flushdns # Windows: clear client cache resolvectl flush-caches # Linux Management tasks: add, change and delete records, set suitable TTLs, keep secondary servers in sync, secure zone transfers (allow only listed secondaries), enable logging, back up zone files, and consider DNSSEC (signs records so answers can be verified)."},{"t":"7.5.4 DNS attacks and protection","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-5-4.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.5.4 DNS attacks and protection Cache poisoning/spoofing, DNS amplification DDoS, tunnelling and hijacking. Protect with DNSSEC, restricted recursion (do not run an open resolver), rate limiting, patching, firewall rules and registrar account security (strong password and multi-factor authentication)."},{"t":"7.6 Obtaining and registering a domain","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-6.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.6 Obtaining and registering a domain Who is involved: ICANN coordinates the namespace; a registry runs each TLD (for example Verisign for .com ; LK Domain Registry for .lk ); a registrar is the company you buy from; the registrant is you. Who is involved in a domain name: ICANN coordinates, a registry runs each TLD, a registrar sells names, and you are the registrant Steps to register: Choose a name: short, memorable, relevant, easy to spell; check trademarks. Check availability with the registrar's search or a whois lookup. Select the TLD ( .com , .org , .lk and so on; some zones have eligibility rules and require documents, for example .ac.lk for academic institutions). Pick an accredited registrar and compare price, renewal cost and support. Provide registrant details (name, address, email, phone) and pay; registration is for 1 to 10 years. Enable protections: WHOIS privacy, auto-renew, registrar lock and two-factor authentication. Set name servers: use the registrar's DNS, a hosting provider's, or your own (create glue records if your name servers are inside the domain, for example ns1.example.com ). Create records: A/AAAA for the website, MX for email, TXT for SPF/DKIM and verification. Wait for propagation: changes may take minutes to 48 hours depending on TTL; verify with dig or an online checker. Renew on time or the domain may expire and be lost. Subdomains (for example portal.example.com ) are free to create once you own the domain. Check yourself. When you have finished this unit, try the 7.7 hands-on lab and the 7.8 review questions ."},{"t":"7.7 Hands-on lab: DNS lookups and a DNS server","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-lab.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.7 Hands-on lab: DNS lookups and a DNS server Do these tasks on your own computer or in the lab using free software. Write down what you see at each step: your notes are useful evidence for assessments and for revision. Use nslookup and dig to find the A, MX and NS records of three websites. Run dig +trace www.example.com and identify the root, TLD and authoritative stages. Build a BIND9 or Windows DNS server for school.lk (or another name you control; avoid .local , which is reserved for multicast DNS) with A, CNAME, MX and PTR records; point a client at it and test. Make a mistake on purpose (wrong serial or missing dot) and fix it using named-checkzone . Look up the registrar and expiry date of a domain with whois ."},{"t":"7.8 Review: key terms and self-check","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07-review.html","x":"Unit 7 · Domain Name System (DNS) 7.8 Review: key terms and self-check Key terms Make sure you can explain each of these in your own words. The glossary has short definitions. DNS FQDN root TLD zone authoritative recursive resolver TTL A/AAAA/CNAME/MX/NS/PTR/SOA/TXT zone transfer DNSSEC registrar registry registrant glue record Self-check questions Write your answers first, then open the answers to check them. What problem does DNS solve? List the components of the FQDN mail.dept.school.lk. Explain the difference between an authoritative and a recursive server. Which record type would you create for an email server and for an alias? What must you change in a zone file after every edit and why? State the steps to register a domain and make a website available on it. Which port and protocol does DNS use, and when does it use TCP? Show answers It translates human-readable names to IP addresses (and other data) so people need not remember numbers. Host mail ; subdomain dept ; second-level domain school ; TLD lk ; root ( . ). An authoritative server holds the official records for a zone; a recursive resolver finds answers by querying other servers and caches them. MX (mail server, together with an A record for the mail host); CNAME (alias). Increase the SOA serial number so secondary servers know the zone changed and transfer it. Choose a name and check that it is available (registrar search or whois ). Select the TLD and an accredited registrar. Register with your details and pay; enable WHOIS privacy, auto-renew, registrar lock and two-factor authentication. Set the name servers (registrar, hosting provider or your own). Create the records: A/AAAA for the website, MX for email, TXT for SPF and verification. Wait for propagation (depends on TTL) and test with dig . Upload the site to the server the A record points to, and renew the domain on time. UDP 53; TCP 53 for large responses and zone transfers. Next: when you are confident with this unit, test yourself with the multiple-choice questions for Unit 7 in the Final Revision section."},{"t":"Unit 7: Domain Name System (DNS)","c":"Unit 7","u":"u07.html","x":"Unit 7 Domain Name System (DNS) The Domain Name System: its hierarchy, how names are resolved, record types, configuring BIND9 and Windows DNS, and registering a domain. What you will learn After this unit you can: Explain the purpose of DNS and describe its hierarchical architecture. Describe the name-resolution process and record types. Install, configure and manage a DNS server. Explain how to obtain and register a domain name. Topics in this unit 7.1 Why DNS? 1 diagram ✓ 7.2 DNS architecture 1 diagram ✓ 7.3 Name resolution step by step 1 diagram ✓ 7.4 DNS record types ✓ 7.5 Configuring a DNS server ✓ 7.5.1 BIND9 on Ubuntu 4 command examples ✓ 7.5.2 Windows Server DNS ✓ 7.5.3 Reverse zones, management and testing 3 command examples · 1 diagram ✓ 7.5.4 DNS attacks and protection ✓ 7.6 Obtaining and registering a domain 1 diagram ✓ 7.7 Hands-on lab: DNS lookups and a DNS server ✓ 7.8 Review: key terms and self-check 7 questions with answers ✓"},{"t":"8.1 Why security settings matter","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-1.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.1 Why security settings matter Servers hold valuable data and run critical services, and they are attacked continuously. Security settings protect the CIA triad : Confidentiality: only authorised people can read data. Integrity: data is not changed without detection. Availability: services are usable when needed. The CIA triad: every security setting protects confidentiality, integrity or availability Common threats: malware and ransomware, phishing, brute-force password attacks, unpatched vulnerabilities, denial of service (DoS/DDoS), man-in-the-middle attacks, misconfiguration and insider misuse. Essential security settings checklist (server hardening) Keep the OS and applications patched ; enable automatic security updates. Remove unneeded software and disable unused services and ports . Use strong passwords and multi-factor authentication; lock out repeated failures. Apply least privilege : normal accounts for daily work, administrator rights only when required; disable direct root/Administrator login over the network. Use SSH keys , change default accounts and passwords on all devices. Enable the host firewall and a network firewall. Use encryption : HTTPS/TLS for services, disk encryption, encrypted backups. Enable logging and monitoring and review logs; synchronise time (NTP) so logs agree. Use antimalware and, where suitable, intrusion detection (IDS/IPS). Keep tested backups (the 3-2-1 rule: 3 copies, 2 media types, 1 off-site). Separate networks (VLANs, DMZ) and restrict management access. Defence in depth: several independent layers of protection, so one failure does not expose the data"},{"t":"8.2 Firewall","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-2.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.2 Firewall A firewall is hardware or software that permits or blocks network traffic according to security rules, acting as a barrier between networks of different trust levels (for example the Internet and the LAN). A firewall sits between networks of different trust and allows or blocks each connection according to its rules In this section 8.2.1 Types of firewall 8.2.2 Rule design, DMZ and ACLs 8.2.3 Configuring firewalls: UFW, Windows and iptables"},{"t":"8.2.1 Types of firewall","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-2-1.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.2.1 Types of firewall Type How it works Notes Packet-filtering Checks source/destination IP, port and protocol of each packet Fast, simple; no knowledge of connection state Stateful inspection Tracks connection state and allows replies to permitted outbound traffic Standard in routers and servers Proxy / application gateway Acts as intermediary and inspects application data Deep control, slower Next-Generation Firewall (NGFW) Adds application awareness, intrusion prevention, user identity, TLS inspection Enterprise networks Web Application Firewall (WAF) Protects web apps from SQL injection, XSS In front of web servers Host-based vs network-based Runs on one computer vs protects a whole network Use both (defence in depth)"},{"t":"8.2.2 Rule design, DMZ and ACLs","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-2-2.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.2.2 Rule design, DMZ and ACLs Firewall rule design Rules are checked top to bottom ; the first match wins. Apply default deny : block everything not explicitly allowed. Allow only required services (for example inbound 80, 443; SSH only from the admin network). Document each rule and review regularly. DMZ (demilitarised zone): a separate network segment for public-facing servers (web, mail) so that if one is compromised the internal LAN is still protected by a second firewall. A DMZ holds public-facing servers between two firewalls, so a compromised web server does not open the internal LAN Access control lists (ACLs) Routers and switches use ACLs to permit or deny packets; for example, a standard ACL filters by source IP only, an extended ACL by source, destination, protocol and port."},{"t":"8.2.3 Configuring firewalls: UFW, Windows and iptables","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-2-3.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.2.3 Configuring firewalls: UFW, Windows and iptables On this page UFW on Ubuntu Windows Defender Firewall iptables on Linux UFW on Ubuntu Terminal Copy sudo ufw default deny incoming sudo ufw default allow outgoing sudo ufw allow 22/tcp # SSH sudo ufw allow 80/tcp # HTTP sudo ufw allow 443/tcp # HTTPS sudo ufw allow from 192.168.1.0/24 to any port 3306 # database only from LAN sudo ufw enable sudo ufw status verbose Windows Defender Firewall Open Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security; create an Inbound Rule > Port > TCP 443 > Allow the connection > choose profiles (Domain/Private/Public) > name the rule. Use New-NetFirewallRule in PowerShell for automation. iptables on Linux Terminal Copy sudo iptables -A INPUT -i lo -j ACCEPT # local traffic sudo iptables -A INPUT -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT # replies sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT # SSH sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT # HTTP sudo iptables -A INPUT -j DROP # everything else sudo iptables -L -n -v --line-numbers Do not lock yourself out. A rule set that only allows port 80 and then drops everything also blocks SSH, local (loopback) traffic and the replies to the server's own connections, so a remote server becomes unreachable. Allow loopback, established connections and SSH first. iptables rules are lost at reboot unless saved (for example with the iptables-persistent package)."},{"t":"8.3 Cryptography basics","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-3.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.3 Cryptography basics Encryption converts readable plaintext into unreadable ciphertext with an algorithm and a key ; decryption reverses it. Security depends on the secrecy of the key, not the algorithm. In this section 8.3.1 Symmetric encryption 8.3.2 Asymmetric (public-key) encryption 8.3.3 Comparison and the hybrid approach 8.3.4 Hashing"},{"t":"8.3.1 Symmetric encryption","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-3-1.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.3.1 Symmetric encryption The same secret key encrypts and decrypts. Symmetric encryption: the same secret key both encrypts and decrypts, so it must be shared safely in advance Algorithms: AES (128/192/256-bit, the current standard), ChaCha20; older DES and 3DES are obsolete. Advantages: very fast, efficient for large data (files, disks, VPN traffic). Disadvantage: the key distribution problem : how do two parties share the key securely? With n users who each need private channels, n(n-1)/2 keys are required."},{"t":"8.3.2 Asymmetric (public-key) encryption","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-3-2.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.3.2 Asymmetric (public-key) encryption A key pair is used: a public key (shared with everyone) and a private key (kept secret). What one key encrypts, only the other can decrypt. Asymmetric encryption: anyone can encrypt with Bob's public key, but only Bob's private key can decrypt Algorithms: RSA (2048-bit or more), ECC (elliptic curves), Diffie-Hellman for key exchange. Encryption for confidentiality: the sender encrypts with the receiver's public key; only the receiver's private key can decrypt. Advantage: solves key distribution; supports digital signatures. Disadvantage: much slower than symmetric encryption."},{"t":"8.3.3 Comparison and the hybrid approach","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-3-3.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.3.3 Comparison and the hybrid approach Comparison Feature Symmetric Asymmetric Keys One shared secret key Public and private key pair Speed Fast Slow Key distribution Difficult Easy (public key can be shared openly) Typical use Bulk data, disk and VPN encryption Key exchange, digital signatures, certificates Examples AES, ChaCha20 RSA, ECC, Diffie-Hellman Hybrid approach (how HTTPS works) Real systems combine both: asymmetric cryptography (and certificates) authenticate the server and securely agree on a session key ; then fast symmetric encryption protects the data. HTTPS combines both: slow asymmetric cryptography agrees a session key, then fast symmetric encryption protects all the data"},{"t":"8.3.4 Hashing","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-3-4.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.3.4 Hashing A hash function (SHA-256, SHA-3) turns any data into a fixed-length digest . It is one-way (cannot be reversed), and any tiny change in the data gives a completely different digest. Uses: file integrity checks, password storage (with a unique salt and a deliberately slow algorithm such as bcrypt or Argon2), and digital signatures. MD5 and SHA-1 are broken and should not be used for security. Real SHA-256 digests: a one-letter change gives a completely different digest, and every digest has the same length Terminal Copy sha256sum file.iso # Linux Get-FileHash file.iso # Windows PowerShell"},{"t":"8.4 Digital signature","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-4.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.4 Digital signature A digital signature proves who created a message (authentication), that it was not altered (integrity) and that the signer cannot deny it (non-repudiation). Signing: the sender (1) computes a hash of the message, (2) encrypts the hash with the sender's private key ; the result is the signature, sent with the message. Verifying: the receiver (1) hashes the received message, (2) decrypts the signature with the sender's public key , and (3) compares the two hashes. If they match, the message is authentic and unchanged. Signing uses the sender's private key; anyone can verify with the sender's public key. Matching digests prove origin and integrity Note the reversal: encryption for secrecy uses the receiver's public key; a signature uses the sender's private key. Task Key used Encrypt a message for secrecy receiver's public key Decrypt the message receiver's private key Sign a message sender's private key Verify a signature sender's public key \"Encrypting the hash with the private key\" describes RSA signatures. Other algorithms, such as ECDSA and Ed25519, calculate the signature differently, but the rule is the same: sign with the private key, verify with the public key."},{"t":"8.5 Digital certificate","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-5.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.5 Digital certificate How do you know a public key really belongs to the claimed owner? A digital certificate (standard X.509 ) binds a public key to an identity (a person, organisation or domain), and is signed by a Certificate Authority (CA) . A certificate contains: subject name (for example www.example.com ), the subject's public key , issuer (the CA), validity dates, serial number, signature algorithm and the CA's digital signature . The main fields of an X.509 certificate. The CA's signature binds the public key to the domain name In this section 8.5.1 PKI and certificate types 8.5.2 How a browser trusts an HTTPS site 8.5.3 Practical: HTTPS certificates"},{"t":"8.5.1 PKI and certificate types","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-5-1.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.5.1 PKI and certificate types Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) PKI is the system of CAs, registration authorities, certificates, revocation lists ( CRL /OCSP) and policies that issues and manages certificates. Browsers and operating systems contain a list of trusted root CAs . A chain of trust runs from the server certificate to an intermediate CA to a trusted root CA. Chain of trust: the browser follows the signatures from the server certificate up to a root CA it already trusts Certificate types Domain Validated (DV): proves control of a domain; quick and cheap (for example Let's Encrypt , which is free). Organisation Validated (OV) / Extended Validation (EV): CA also verifies the organisation. Self-signed: signed by itself; fine for labs and internal testing, but browsers show warnings. Wildcard ( *.example.com ) and multi-domain (SAN) certificates."},{"t":"8.5.2 How a browser trusts an HTTPS site","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-5-2.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.5.2 How a browser trusts an HTTPS site The browser connects to the server on port 443 and says which TLS versions and ciphers it supports. The server sends its certificate . The browser checks the CA signature and chain, the validity dates , and that the certificate name matches the domain . The browser and server agree on a session key using asymmetric key exchange. Both switch to symmetric encryption (for example AES) for the rest of the session. Simplified TLS handshake: the server proves its identity with a certificate, both sides agree a session key, then all data is encrypted"},{"t":"8.5.3 Practical: HTTPS certificates","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-5-3.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.5.3 Practical: HTTPS certificates Practical: enable HTTPS with Let's Encrypt (Ubuntu + Apache) Terminal Copy sudo apt install certbot python3-certbot-apache -y sudo certbot --apache -d www.example.com sudo certbot renew --dry-run This requires a real domain pointing to your server and ports 80/443 open. Practical: create and inspect a self-signed certificate Terminal Copy openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -keyout key.pem -out cert.pem -days 365 openssl x509 -in cert.pem -text -noout Check yourself. When you have finished this unit, try the 8.6 hands-on lab and the 8.7 review questions ."},{"t":"8.6 Hands-on lab: Firewalls and cryptography","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-lab.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.6 Hands-on lab: Firewalls and cryptography Do these tasks on your own computer or in the lab using free software. Write down what you see at each step: your notes are useful evidence for assessments and for revision. Ethics: scan, test and attack only machines in your own lab. Scanning other people's systems without written permission is illegal in many countries. Enable UFW on an Ubuntu VM with default deny; allow only SSH and HTTP; test from another machine with nmap (own lab only). Create a firewall rule on Windows to block ping (ICMP), test, then remove it. Encrypt a file with a password using openssl enc -aes-256-cbc -salt -pbkdf2 -in a.txt -out a.enc , then decrypt it. Generate an RSA key pair, sign a file with the private key, and verify with the public key ( openssl dgst -sha256 -sign and -verify ). Open a HTTPS site in a browser, view the certificate and write down issuer, validity and the chain. Terminal Copy # encrypt and decrypt a file with a password (AES-256) openssl enc -aes-256-cbc -salt -pbkdf2 -in a.txt -out a.enc openssl enc -d -aes-256-cbc -pbkdf2 -in a.enc -out a-decrypted.txt # create a key pair, sign a file and verify the signature openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048 -out private.pem openssl pkey -in private.pem -pubout -out public.pem openssl dgst -sha256 -sign private.pem -out a.sig a.txt openssl dgst -sha256 -verify public.pem -signature a.sig a.txt"},{"t":"8.7 Review: key terms and self-check","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08-review.html","x":"Unit 8 · Network Security 8.7 Review: key terms and self-check Key terms Make sure you can explain each of these in your own words. The glossary has short definitions. CIA triad hardening least privilege firewall stateful DMZ ACL plaintext ciphertext symmetric asymmetric public key private key AES RSA hash digital signature certificate CA PKI TLS Self-check questions Write your answers first, then open the answers to check them. List four security settings you would apply to a new server and explain why. What does \"default deny\" mean in a firewall? Compare symmetric and asymmetric encryption (any three points). Which key does a sender use to create a digital signature, and which key does the receiver use to verify it? What problem does a digital certificate solve, and who signs it? Explain why HTTPS uses both asymmetric and symmetric cryptography. What is the purpose of a DMZ? Show answers Any four, with reasons, for example: Apply all updates: fixes known vulnerabilities that attackers scan for. Disable unused services and ports: reduces the attack surface. Strong passwords or SSH keys with MFA, no direct root login: stops brute-force and stolen-password attacks. Host firewall with default deny: only required services are reachable. Logging and monitoring with NTP time: incidents are detected and can be investigated. Tested backups (3-2-1): data can be recovered after ransomware or failure. Everything is blocked unless a rule explicitly allows it. Symmetric uses one shared key, is fast and is used for bulk data; asymmetric uses a public/private key pair, is slower and is used for key exchange and signatures; symmetric has a key-distribution problem which asymmetric solves. The sender's private key signs; the receiver uses the sender's public key to verify. It proves that a public key belongs to a named owner, preventing impersonation; it is signed by a trusted Certificate Authority. Asymmetric cryptography authenticates the server and securely agrees a key, then faster symmetric encryption protects the bulk data. A DMZ isolates public-facing servers from the internal LAN so a compromised server does not give direct access to internal systems. Next: when you are confident with this unit, test yourself with the multiple-choice questions for Unit 8 in the Final Revision section."},{"t":"Unit 8: Network Security","c":"Unit 8","u":"u08.html","x":"Unit 8 Network Security Protecting servers and networks: hardening, firewalls and DMZs, symmetric and asymmetric encryption, hashing, digital signatures and certificates. What you will learn After this unit you can: Explain the significance of security settings on servers and networks. Describe the types of firewall and configure basic firewall rules. Explain symmetric and asymmetric encryption and where each is used. Explain hashing, digital signatures and digital certificates. Topics in this unit 8.1 Why security settings matter 2 diagrams ✓ 8.2 Firewall 1 diagram ✓ 8.2.1 Types of firewall ✓ 8.2.2 Rule design, DMZ and ACLs 1 diagram ✓ 8.2.3 Configuring firewalls: UFW, Windows and iptables 2 command examples ✓ 8.3 Cryptography basics ✓ 8.3.1 Symmetric encryption 1 diagram ✓ 8.3.2 Asymmetric (public-key) encryption 1 diagram ✓ 8.3.3 Comparison and the hybrid approach 1 diagram ✓ 8.3.4 Hashing 1 command example · 1 diagram ✓ 8.4 Digital signature 1 diagram ✓ 8.5 Digital certificate 1 diagram ✓ 8.5.1 PKI and certificate types 1 diagram ✓ 8.5.2 How a browser trusts an HTTPS site 1 diagram ✓ 8.5.3 Practical: HTTPS certificates 2 command examples ✓ 8.6 Hands-on lab: Firewalls and cryptography 1 command example ✓ 8.7 Review: key terms and self-check 7 questions with answers ✓"},{"t":"9.1 Troubleshooting methodology","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09-1.html","x":"Unit 9 · Network Troubleshooting 9.1 Troubleshooting methodology 1 Identify the problem → 2 Theory of cause → 3 Test the theory → 4 Plan of action → 5 Implement → 6 Verify → 7 Document Identify the problem: gather information, ask questions (who, what, when, what changed?), reproduce the fault, define the scope (one user, one subnet, everyone). Establish a theory of probable cause (start with the simple and common). Test the theory to confirm; if wrong, form a new theory or escalate. Establish a plan of action and consider the impact (get approval for production changes). Implement the solution (change only one thing at a time). Verify full system functionality and apply preventive measures. Document findings, actions and outcomes. Approaches Top-down: start at the application layer and move down. Bottom-up: start at the physical layer and move up (best when nothing works). Divide and conquer: start at a middle layer (usually ping at layer 3) and move up or down depending on the result. Follow the path and compare with a working system ; substitute known-good parts. Three ways to work through the layers: top-down, bottom-up, or divide and conquer starting at layer 3 with ping"},{"t":"9.2 Layer-by-layer symptoms","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09-2.html","x":"Unit 9 · Network Troubleshooting 9.2 Layer-by-layer symptoms Layer Typical faults Checks 1 Physical Unplugged or faulty cable, dead port, no power, damaged fibre Link lights, swap cable, cable tester, check port 2 Data link Wrong VLAN, duplex mismatch, MAC filtering, loop show interfaces , VLAN membership, switch MAC table 3 Network Wrong IP, mask or gateway, duplicate IP, routing fault, APIPA address ipconfig , ping , tracert , routing table 4 Transport Port blocked by firewall, service not listening netstat / ss , telnet host port , firewall rules 5-7 Application Service stopped, misconfiguration, certificate expired, DNS error Service status, application logs, browser errors"},{"t":"9.3 Command-line tools","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09-3.html","x":"Unit 9 · Network Troubleshooting 9.3 Command-line tools Tool Windows Linux Use Show IP settings ipconfig /all ip a , ip route Check IP, mask, gateway, DNS, MAC Renew address ipconfig /release and /renew sudo dhclient -r; sudo dhclient (or sudo networkctl renew <interface> ) DHCP faults Test reachability ping ping Layer 3 connectivity and latency Trace path tracert traceroute Find where packets stop Path quality pathping mtr Loss at each hop DNS lookup nslookup dig , nslookup Test name resolution Connections and ports netstat -ano ss -tulpn See open ports, processes ARP table arp -a ip neigh IP-to-MAC mapping, duplicate IP Port test Test-NetConnection host -Port 443 nc -zv host 443 Is the service reachable? Capture traffic Wireshark tcpdump , Wireshark Deep analysis Web test browser, curl curl -I http://site Check HTTP response Service status Services, Get-Service systemctl status name Is the application running? Logs Event Viewer journalctl -xe , /var/log/* Find the error message Resources Task Manager, Get-Process top , free -h , df -h CPU, RAM, disk full Interpreting ping results Result Meaning Reply from address Layer 3 path is working Request timed out Host down, ICMP blocked, or no route back Destination host unreachable No route or ARP failure; check IP, mask and gateway General failure / transmit failed Local interface or driver problem High or variable time Congestion, wireless interference, overloaded device"},{"t":"9.4 The \"cannot reach the Internet\" sequence","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09-4.html","x":"Unit 9 · Network Troubleshooting 9.4 The \"cannot reach the Internet\" sequence The standard sequence works outwards from your own computer, so the first test that fails shows where the fault is ping 127.0.0.1 - is the TCP/IP software working on this computer? ipconfig /all - is the IP address valid (not 169.254.x.x)? Is the gateway correct? ping the default gateway - is the local network working? ping 8.8.8.8 - is the Internet reachable by IP? ping www.google.com - does DNS work? If this fails while step 4 works, the fault is DNS . tracert 8.8.8.8 - where does the path stop?"},{"t":"9.5 Common faults and fixes","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09-5.html","x":"Unit 9 · Network Troubleshooting 9.5 Common faults and fixes Symptom Likely cause Fix 169.254.x.x address DHCP server unreachable Check cable/Wi-Fi, DHCP service and scope; renew lease Can ping IP but not name DNS problem Check client DNS settings, DNS server status, nslookup , flush cache Can reach local hosts, not Internet Wrong or missing gateway, ISP fault Correct gateway, check router and WAN link Intermittent connection Bad cable, Wi-Fi interference, duplex mismatch Replace cable, change channel, set speed/duplex IP conflict message Two devices with same IP Use DHCP reservations, find the device with arp -a Website down, server pings fine Web service stopped or port blocked systemctl status , check firewall and port 80/443 Cannot SSH or RDP Service off, firewall, wrong port or credentials Check service, ss -tulpn , firewall rules, logs Certificate warning Expired or mismatched certificate, wrong date on client Renew certificate, check name and system clock Server slow CPU, RAM or disk exhausted, too many connections top , df -h , find the process, add resources Server will not boot Bad disk, corrupt update, wrong boot order Boot recovery media, check disk health, restore from backup Application crashes after update Incompatible version, missing dependency Read logs, roll back, reinstall dependency Cannot send email Wrong MX/SMTP settings, port 25 blocked, SPF failure Check DNS records, mail logs, firewall"},{"t":"9.6 Operating system and application troubleshooting","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09-6.html","x":"Unit 9 · Network Troubleshooting 9.6 Operating system and application troubleshooting 1 Read the logs → 2 Check config syntax → 3 Check resources → 4 Check permissions → 5 Check recent changes Check the logs first: the exact error message usually points to the cause. On Linux use sudo journalctl -u apache2 --since \"1 hour ago\" and tail -f /var/log/syslog ; on Windows open Event Viewer (System and Application). Check the configuration syntax before restarting a service ( apache2ctl configtest , named-checkconf , nginx -t ). Check resources: disk full ( df -h ) is a very common cause of strange failures. Check permissions and ownership of files and users. Check recent changes: updates, new software, configuration edits. Roll back if needed. Boot problems: use Safe Mode or Recovery Environment (Windows), rescue mode or GRUB recovery (Linux); restore from backup if the system is damaged. Keep baseline records of normal performance so abnormal behaviour is easy to recognise."},{"t":"9.7 Documentation","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09-7.html","x":"Unit 9 · Network Troubleshooting 9.7 Documentation Write a trouble ticket : date, reporter, symptom, affected systems, tests performed, root cause, fix, time to resolve and follow-up action. A knowledge base of past fixes saves time later. Sample trouble ticket Field Example Ticket / date #0142 · 14 March 2026, 10:20 Reported by Accounts office, PC ACC-03 Symptom Cannot open websites; email in Outlook still works Affected systems One PC only Tests performed ping 8.8.8.8 succeeds; ping www.google.com fails; ipconfig /all shows DNS server 192.168.1.100 Root cause DNS server address typed incorrectly (should be 192.168.1.10) Fix Set the adapter back to obtain DNS automatically (DHCP); ipconfig /flushdns Time to resolve 15 minutes Follow-up Remove local administrator rights so users cannot change network settings Check yourself. When you have finished this unit, try the 9.8 hands-on lab and the 9.9 review questions ."},{"t":"9.8 Hands-on lab: Fault-finding exercise","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09-lab.html","x":"Unit 9 · Network Troubleshooting 9.8 Hands-on lab: Fault-finding exercise Your teacher or a partner breaks one thing on a working lab network. Find and fix each fault using the method above and write a ticket for each: Wrong subnet mask on a PC. Wrong default gateway. DNS server address typed incorrectly. Web service stopped on the server. Firewall rule blocking port 80. Disconnected or wrong-VLAN port. Disk full or stopped service on a Linux server. Use the method: follow the \"cannot reach the Internet\" sequence for each fault and record it in a ticket like the sample in 9.7 ."},{"t":"9.9 Review: key terms and self-check","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09-review.html","x":"Unit 9 · Network Troubleshooting 9.9 Review: key terms and self-check Key terms Make sure you can explain each of these in your own words. The glossary has short definitions. Top-down bottom-up divide and conquer ping traceroute nslookup netstat log baseline root cause ticket Self-check questions Write your answers first, then open the answers to check them. List the steps of the CompTIA-style troubleshooting method in order. A user can ping 8.8.8.8 but cannot open websites by name. What is the likely fault and how do you confirm it? Which tool shows where along a path packets stop? A Linux web server is not responding. Name five checks you would do. What does an IP address of 169.254.12.5 on a client indicate? Why should you change only one thing at a time? Show answers Identify the problem (gather information, reproduce, define the scope). Establish a theory of probable cause. Test the theory to determine the cause. Establish a plan of action and identify its impact. Implement the solution (or escalate). Verify full system functionality and apply preventive measures. Document findings, actions and outcomes. DNS failure; confirm with nslookup www.example.com and by changing the DNS server (for example to 8.8.8.8). tracert (Windows) or traceroute (Linux). Any five, for example: ping the server and check its IP settings ( ip a ). systemctl status apache2 to see whether the web service is running. ss -tulpn to check it is listening on port 80/443. sudo ufw status to check the firewall allows the port. journalctl -u apache2 and /var/log/apache2/error.log for error messages; apache2ctl configtest for syntax. df -h , free -h and top for a full disk, low memory or high CPU. nslookup / dig to check the name resolves to the right address. The client failed to get a DHCP address and self-assigned an APIPA address. So you can tell which change fixed the problem or caused a new one and can easily undo it. Next: when you are confident with this unit, test yourself with the multiple-choice questions for Unit 9 in the Final Revision section."},{"t":"Unit 9: Network Troubleshooting","c":"Unit 9","u":"u09.html","x":"Unit 9 Network Troubleshooting A systematic way to find and fix faults: the troubleshooting method, layer-by-layer symptoms, command-line tools, common faults and documenting the fix. What you will learn After this unit you can: Apply a systematic troubleshooting method. Use command-line tools to diagnose network, DNS and server problems. Troubleshoot operating system, application and network issues using logs and tools. Document faults and solutions. Topics in this unit 9.1 Troubleshooting methodology 1 diagram ✓ 9.2 Layer-by-layer symptoms ✓ 9.3 Command-line tools ✓ 9.4 The \"cannot reach the Internet\" sequence 1 diagram ✓ 9.5 Common faults and fixes ✓ 9.6 Operating system and application troubleshooting ✓ 9.7 Documentation ✓ 9.8 Hands-on lab: Fault-finding exercise ✓ 9.9 Review: key terms and self-check 6 questions with answers ✓"},{"t":"Final Revision and Self-Assessment","c":"Final Revision","u":"revision.html","x":"Final Revision Final Revision and Self-Assessment Check your understanding of the whole module with 100 multiple-choice questions, 100 structured questions and a topic checklist. How to use this section Work through the questions after finishing all the units. Every answer links back to the topic it tests, so use your wrong answers as a revision list. Topics in this section R.1 Multiple-Choice Questions ✓ R.2 Structured Questions ✓ R.3 Topic Checklist ✓"},{"t":"R.1 Multiple-Choice Questions","c":"Final Revision","u":"revision-mcq.html","x":"practice questions answers quiz exam revision"},{"t":"R.2 Structured Questions","c":"Final Revision","u":"revision-structured.html","x":"practice questions answers quiz exam revision"},{"t":"R.3 Topic Checklist","c":"Final Revision","u":"revision-checklist.html","x":"Final Revision and Self-Assessment R.3 Topic Checklist Tick each outcome when you can explain it and do it without looking at your notes. Your ticks are saved in this browser. Each link opens the unit. Unit 1: Data Communication Fundamentals Define data communication and list its five components. Unit 1 Distinguish transmission modes, data flow directions and signal types. Unit 1 Compare transmission media. Unit 1 Explain bandwidth, throughput, latency and the basic ideas of multiplexing and switching. Unit 1 Unit 2: Computer Network Fundamentals Define a computer network and describe its purposes. Unit 2 Classify networks by size (PAN, LAN, MAN, WAN) and by role (peer-to-peer, client-server). Unit 2 Compare physical and logical topologies. Unit 2 Explain the Internet, intranets and extranets. Unit 2 Describe wired and wireless LAN technologies and basic network design. Unit 2 Unit 3: Network Devices and Protocols Identify common network devices and state the function of each. Unit 3 Install and configure a switch, router, access point and basic firewall. Unit 3 Explain what a network protocol is and describe the most common protocols. Unit 3 Describe the OSI and TCP/IP reference models and map devices and protocols to layers. Unit 3 Unit 4: Addressing Network Devices Describe the structure and purpose of a MAC address. Unit 4 Explain IPv4 and IPv6 addressing, classes, public and private ranges. Unit 4 Convert between binary and decimal. Unit 4 Design and calculate subnets using subnet masks, CIDR and VLSM. Unit 4 Unit 5: Server Installation, Setting Up and Configuration Explain what a server is and why server operating systems and application software are required. Unit 5 Compare dedicated, shared, VPS, cloud and virtual servers and select a suitable one. Unit 5 Install and configure a server operating system. Unit 5 Describe and use server access methods. Unit 5 Install common server applications and tools. Unit 5 Explain ports and manage them securely. Unit 5 Unit 6: Virtual Private Network (VPN) Explain what a VPN is and why it is used. Unit 6 Compare site-to-site and remote-access VPNs. Unit 6 Describe tunnelling and common VPN protocols. Unit 6 Set up a simple VPN. Unit 6 Unit 7: Domain Name System (DNS) Explain the purpose of DNS and describe its hierarchical architecture. Unit 7 Describe the name-resolution process and record types. Unit 7 Install, configure and manage a DNS server. Unit 7 Explain how to obtain and register a domain name. Unit 7 Unit 8: Network Security Explain the significance of security settings on servers and networks. Unit 8 Describe the types of firewall and configure basic firewall rules. Unit 8 Explain symmetric and asymmetric encryption and where each is used. Unit 8 Explain hashing, digital signatures and digital certificates. Unit 8 Unit 9: Network Troubleshooting Apply a systematic troubleshooting method. Unit 9 Use command-line tools to diagnose network, DNS and server problems. Unit 9 Troubleshoot operating system, application and network issues using logs and tools. Unit 9 Document faults and solutions. Unit 9"},{"t":"Appendix A Glossary","c":"Appendices","u":"appendix-a.html","x":"Appendices A Glossary Key terms used in this module, in alphabetical order. Term Meaning ACL Access Control List: ordered permit/deny rules on a router or switch AES Advanced Encryption Standard; the common symmetric cipher APIPA Automatic Private IP Addressing: a 169.254.x.x address a client gives itself when DHCP fails ARP Address Resolution Protocol; maps IP to MAC on a LAN Bandwidth Maximum data capacity of a link Broadcast domain Part of a network reached by a broadcast frame; routers separate broadcast domains CA Certificate Authority; trusted body that signs certificates CIDR Classless Inter-Domain Routing; prefix notation such as /24 Collision domain Part of a network where frames can collide; each switch port is its own collision domain CSMA/CA Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Avoidance, used by Wi-Fi CSMA/CD Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Detection, used by early half-duplex Ethernet Default gateway The router interface a host sends traffic to for any destination outside its own subnet DHCP Protocol that automatically assigns IP settings Digital certificate An X.509 document, signed by a CA, that binds a public key to an identity Digital signature A value created with the sender's private key that proves origin and integrity DMZ Network zone for public-facing servers DNS System that translates names to IP addresses DNSSEC DNS Security Extensions: signs DNS records so answers can be verified DORA Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge: the four DHCP messages Encapsulation Adding a header (and trailer) at each layer as data moves down the stack Firewall Device or software filtering traffic by rules FQDN Fully Qualified Domain Name Gateway Router that links a network to others Hash Fixed-length one-way digest of data Hypervisor Software that runs virtual machines IPsec Network-layer VPN and security protocol suite Latency The time data takes to travel from source to destination MAC address 48-bit hardware address of a network interface NAT Translates private addresses to public ones OSI model Seven-layer reference model for communication OUI Organisationally Unique Identifier: the first 24 bits of a MAC address, identifying the manufacturer Packet Layer 3 data unit PDU Protocol Data Unit: the data unit at a layer (segment, packet, frame, bits) PKI Public Key Infrastructure; system managing certificates Port 16-bit number identifying a service on a host RAID Combining disks for speed and/or redundancy Recursive resolver DNS server that finds an answer for a client by querying other servers, then caches it Registrar Company that sells domain names RSA Widely used asymmetric algorithm SLAAC Stateless Address Autoconfiguration: IPv6 hosts build their own address from the router's prefix Socket An IP address plus a port number, for example 192.168.1.10:443 SSH Secure Shell; encrypted remote access Subnet mask Separates network and host parts of an IP address TCP / UDP Reliable / unreliable transport protocols Throughput The data rate actually achieved on a link, always at or below the bandwidth TLS Transport Layer Security; encrypts HTTPS and many other protocols Topology The physical or logical arrangement of devices and links in a network Trunk A switch link that carries several VLANs, with 802.1Q tags TTL Time To Live: how long a DNS answer may be cached VLAN Logical network on a switch VLSM Variable Length Subnet Masking VPN Virtual Private Network VPS Virtual Private Server Zone Part of the DNS namespace managed by one set of servers"},{"t":"Appendix B Quick Reference Cards","c":"Appendices","u":"appendix-b.html","x":"Appendices B Quick Reference Cards On this page Subnetting cheat sheet Private and special IPv4 addresses Common port numbers Which key for which job Troubleshooting order Everyday commands Subnetting cheat sheet Prefix Mask Block size Usable hosts /8 255.0.0.0 - 16,777,214 /16 255.255.0.0 - 65,534 /23 255.255.254.0 2 (third octet) 510 /24 255.255.255.0 1 (third octet) 254 /25 255.255.255.128 128 126 /26 255.255.255.192 64 62 /27 255.255.255.224 32 30 /28 255.255.255.240 16 14 /29 255.255.255.248 8 6 /30 255.255.255.252 4 2 Formulas: usable hosts = 2^h − 2 · subnets = 2^s · block size = 256 − mask octet. Powers of two: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256. Practise with the subnet calculator and the VLSM planner . Private and special IPv4 addresses Range Use 10.0.0.0/8 Private (RFC 1918) 172.16.0.0/12 Private (172.16.0.0 to 172.31.255.255) 192.168.0.0/16 Private 127.0.0.0/8 Loopback (127.0.0.1) 169.254.0.0/16 APIPA: DHCP failed 224.0.0.0/4 Multicast Common port numbers Port Service Port Service 20/21 FTP 143 IMAP 22 SSH, SFTP, SCP 161 SNMP (UDP) 23 Telnet (insecure) 389 / 636 LDAP / LDAPS 25 / 587 SMTP 443 HTTPS 53 DNS (UDP, TCP) 445 SMB 67/68 DHCP (UDP) 3306 MySQL 80 HTTP 3389 RDP 110 POP3 51820 WireGuard (UDP) Which key for which job Task Key used Encrypt for secrecy receiver's public key Decrypt receiver's private key Sign sender's private key Verify a signature sender's public key Troubleshooting order 1 ping 127.0.0.1 → 2 ipconfig /all → 3 ping gateway → 4 ping 8.8.8.8 → 5 ping a name → 6 tracert Everyday commands Task Windows Linux IP settings ipconfig /all ip a , ip route Reachability ping ping Path tracert traceroute , mtr DNS lookup nslookup dig , nslookup Listening ports netstat -ano ss -tulpn ARP table arp -a ip neigh Port test Test-NetConnection host -Port 443 nc -zv host 443 Services Get-Service systemctl status name Logs Event Viewer journalctl -xe , /var/log"},{"t":"Appendix C Lab Set-up and Study Plan","c":"Appendices","u":"appendix-c.html","x":"Appendices C Lab Set-up and Study Plan On this page Recommended lab set-up Self-study roadmap A routine for each unit Recommended lab set-up Everything in this module can be practised with free software on one computer: A computer with at least 8 GB RAM and virtualization enabled. VirtualBox or VMware Player to run virtual machines. Ubuntu Server LTS ISO and a Windows Server evaluation ISO. Cisco Packet Tracer (free from Cisco Networking Academy) for simulating routers and switches. Wireshark for packet capture. PuTTY or the built-in ssh client. Study tip: do the labs. Networking and servers are practical skills; reading alone is not enough. Each unit ends with a hands-on lab page. Self-study roadmap A seven-week plan for working through the module on your own: Week Study Lab to complete 1 Units 1 and 2 Labs 1 and 2 2 Unit 3 Lab 3 3 Unit 4 (practise 20 subnetting problems daily) Lab 4 4 Unit 5 Lab 5 5 Units 6 and 7 Labs 6 and 7 6 Unit 8 Lab 8 7 Unit 9 and revision Lab 9, full mock test A student who reads each unit, completes its lab and answers the self-check questions has covered every point of the module. A routine for each unit Read the unit overview and its learning outcomes. Work through each short topic page, trying every command and interactive lab. Complete the hands-on lab and keep notes or screenshots. Answer the review questions before opening the answers. Tick the outcomes in the topic checklist ."},{"t":"Appendix D References and Free Resources","c":"Appendices","u":"appendix-d.html","x":"Appendices D References and Free Resources On this page Free online resources Books Software used in the labs Free online resources Cisco Networking Academy: free courses (Networking Basics) and Packet Tracer Ubuntu Server documentation Microsoft Learn: Windows Server documentation BIND 9 Administrator Reference Manual Wireshark User's Guide and sample captures Let's Encrypt documentation ICANN (domain name system) IANA root zone and port number registry WireGuard quick start The Linux Command Line by William Shotts (free online book) Books Kurose, J. and Ross, K. Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach . Pearson. Tanenbaum, A. and Wetherall, D. Computer Networks . Pearson. CompTIA Network+ and CompTIA Server+ study guides. Shotts, W. The Linux Command Line . No Starch Press (free online edition). Software used in the labs Software Used for VirtualBox or VMware Player Running server virtual machines Ubuntu Server LTS Linux server labs (SSH, Apache, BIND9, UFW, WireGuard) Windows Server evaluation Server Manager, DNS and DHCP roles, Windows Firewall Cisco Packet Tracer Simulating switches, routers and VLANs Wireshark Capturing and analysing packets PuTTY or the built-in ssh client Remote command-line access OpenSSL Encryption, signatures and certificates (included with Linux and Git for Windows)"},{"t":"Appendices","c":"Appendices","u":"appendices.html","x":"Appendices Appendices Glossary, quick reference cards, your lab set-up and study plan, and free resources for further study. Topics in this section Appendix A Glossary ✓ Appendix B Quick Reference Cards ✓ Appendix C Lab Set-up and Study Plan ✓ Appendix D References and Free Resources ✓"}];
